      <data key="d0">Weka Baseline</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A standard machine learning baseline model against which the proposed neural model was benchmarked during the WASSA-2017 competition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe22bfea47113bef8076ac67e7587b69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Joy Dataset">
      <data key="d0">Joy Dataset</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An emotion-specific benchmark dataset used to test model correlation gains, embedding sizes, and token attention weight distributions for positive words.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe22bfea47113bef8076ac67e7587b69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anger Dataset">
      <data key="d0">Anger Dataset</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A benchmark dataset used in the WASSA-2017 evaluation to determine the correlation impact of various GloVe embedding sizes and RNN hidden layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe22bfea47113bef8076ac67e7587b69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fear Dataset">
      <data key="d0">Fear Dataset</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A benchmark dataset applied in experiments testing GloVe vector sizes and RNN hidden state configurations for emotion intensity prediction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe22bfea47113bef8076ac67e7587b69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sadness Dataset">
      <data key="d0">Sadness Dataset</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An emotion benchmark dataset evaluated to measure the correlation gains of different embedding dimensions and RNN unit sizes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe22bfea47113bef8076ac67e7587b69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lexical And Syntactic Ambiguity">
      <data key="d0">Lexical And Syntactic Ambiguity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A prominent natural language obstacle causing translation errors that Semantic Web technologies are targeted to resolve.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe22bfea47113bef8076ac67e7587b69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rule-Based Machine Translation">
      <data key="d0">Rule-Based Machine Translation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A machine translation methodology based on linguistics and manually crafted rules, which faces challenges in scalability and multi-language adaptation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Statistical Machine Translation">
      <data key="d0">Statistical Machine Translation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A machine translation paradigm developed to address scalability limitations of rule-based systems, but prone to ambiguity issues across different domains.&lt;SEP&gt;A machine translation paradigm that models translation using statistical methods, often applied with subword units for low-resource related languages.&lt;SEP&gt;A translation approach (SMT) where subword level models are applied between related languages to alleviate word-level translation issues.&lt;SEP&gt;A translation approach represented in the study by a string-to-tree system (SBMT) used as a competitive baseline and re-scored using transfer NMT models.&lt;SEP&gt;A translation framework in which Byte Pair Encoding and other subword units were evaluated for translation between related languages.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical Machine Translation (SMT) is a machine translation approach where translations are generated based on statistical models derived from the analysis of bilingual text corpora.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical Machine Translation (SMT) is an approach to automated translation evaluated across various subword units, training sizes, and domain changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical Machine Translation (SMT) is an approach to automated translation that relies on statistical models trained on bilingual text corpora.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical Machine Translation (SMT) is an approach to machine translation based on statistical analysis of bilingual text corpora.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical Machine Translation (SMT) is an approach to machine translation where translations are generated based on statistical models derived from bilingual text corpora.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical Machine Translation is a machine translation paradigm relying on statistical models trained on large corpora to generate translations.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical Machine Translation represents an earlier paradigm of machine translation referenced in prior studies on Southern African languages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea126b0da9c38f85b697b36cd33629f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddef736560bcb899db81b75205f4df60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2af0dac5bc5afdea73673fd2462c1e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba3fa25c5e29c7ea3201eac4d5fbec61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acd45458d7c3c63e9891f23252405a17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-762be16cab89cb90aaf53d4505452bf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-116526d304daa442dbc1df519e7f8fc5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffaa7abbd8709e94a9523e6b922ac96b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fff775482c4d8a3088b428b10d9f761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Example-Based Machine Translation">
      <data key="d0">Example-Based Machine Translation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An MT approach developed alongside SMT to tackle scalability issues present in rule-based machine translation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Semantic Web Technologies">
      <data key="d0">Semantic Web Technologies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of technologies and standards that structure web data, utilized in research to address entity ambiguity and out-of-vocabulary challenges in translation.&lt;SEP&gt;Semantic Web Technologies (SWT) encompass standards, ontologies, and frameworks such as RDF and SPARQL used for managing structured, linked data.&lt;SEP&gt;Technologies designed to make content semantics explicit for machines, studied as an approach to resolve ambiguity and improve machine translation quality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3bf41e0b7ea425266139faf090ad226</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Knowledge Base">
      <data key="d0">Knowledge Base</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Knowledge Base (KB) is a structured repository of information, entities, and relationships used to improve translation accuracy and contextual understanding.&lt;SEP&gt;A Knowledge Base is a repository of structured information from which facts are retrieved to inform question-answering neural models.&lt;SEP&gt;A knowledge base is an organized repository of structured facts and entities used by neural models to retrieve evidence for answering complex queries.&lt;SEP&gt;A repository of structured or unstructured facts, including commonsense assertions and knowledge graphs, used to ground dialogue systems and inform conversational agents.&lt;SEP&gt;A structured repository of entity relations and facts (KB) used to train, evaluate, or enhance relation extraction models.&lt;SEP&gt;An organized repository of information and facts used by reasoning systems and neural networks to answer questions and inform recommendations.&lt;SEP&gt;Structured factual data organized in triple format from which relevant facts are retrieved to assist in answering queries.&lt;SEP&gt;Structured representations of real-world entities, properties, and relationships utilized in MT systems to extract and preserve semantic meaning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b32cb050de56f58b5792720c6bc3e42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdef2f67b0ab332d8da79c41fb0e58c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7d53ab63937232bbcc4be9c7080dd31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10ce525fd225c14101bb62983a2ac21a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c53d0ac7fa47d30ce6f399d3ef8b95e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-362346ad5b3a18dced3a68b0e282ea62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BabelNet">
      <data key="d0">BabelNet</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A multilingual semantic network and knowledge base used in MT architectures to handle out-of-vocabulary (OOV) words.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Knight">
      <data key="d0">Knight</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who pioneered the idea of utilizing structured knowledge bases in machine translation systems in the 1990s alongside Luk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Luk">
      <data key="d0">Luk</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who collaborated with Knight in the 1990s on integrating structured knowledge bases into machine translation systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Du">
      <data key="d0">Du</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who developed an approach using BabelNet to address out-of-vocabulary words in machine translation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shi">
      <data key="d0">Shi</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who designed a semantic embedding model using knowledge base triples for neural machine translation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lopez">
      <data key="d0">Lopez</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who, together with Post, identified five prominent open challenges in machine translation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Post">
      <data key="d0">Post</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who, together with Lopez, articulated five key open challenges faced by modern machine translation systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Union">
      <data key="d0">European Union</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A political and economic union where the use and processing of personal health data for scientific research is regulated by the GDPR.&lt;SEP&gt;A supranational political and economic union that provided funding for the MeMAD and FoTran research projects through its Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme.&lt;SEP&gt;An international governing entity whose extensive legal and legislative documentation provides training data for machine translation systems.&lt;SEP&gt;An international organization whose research projects (SIMPOL, MULTIPLEX, and DOLFINS) partially supported the study.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Union is a political and economic union whose official legislative texts, such as the JRC-Acquis corpus and EU Bookshop publications, provide training data for language identification.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Union is a supranational political and economic union whose Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme funded the research described in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Union is an international political and economic union that provided funding for the research through its Horizon 2020 programme.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e9d6cd8b1a1f2b1ece006c42d145e4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43d472bd5e8478be39954bdc44e0469e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b470fa40233ba7e36a9f7c70bafce9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e8fa58e562bb861dae62114ff9c810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7562690242fd78972d67afd148f518b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d0343896fcb9bca626f6a9369daf5a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Linked Data Web">
      <data key="d0">Linked Data Web</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Web of interconnected, structured data offering disambiguated knowledge about real-world entities, properties, and relationships that can be leveraged by machines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Knowledge Graph">
      <data key="d0">Knowledge Graph</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A structured representation of knowledge and relationships that has gained renewed interest for integration into MT systems to improve semantic accuracy.&lt;SEP&gt;A structured representation of real-world facts and relationships incorporated into event embeddings to improve predictive performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9d3fbec1ae6ff5c0cac8214c21ddda4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Out-of-Vocabulary Words">
      <data key="d0">Out-of-Vocabulary Words</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Words not present in the training vocabulary of an MT system, posing translation challenges that techniques such as BabelNet queries aim to resolve.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neural Network Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Neural Network Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Computational learning algorithms underlying modern NMT systems that enable high translation performance and effective word reordering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Speech Translation">
      <data key="d0">Speech Translation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A machine learning and speech processing domain focusing on converting speech in a source language into text in a target language.&lt;SEP&gt;A subfield of translation focused on translating spoken language, facing open challenges due to differences between training parallel data and real user speech.&lt;SEP&gt;Speech translation is a subfield of artificial intelligence and natural language processing involving the translation of spoken audio in one language into text in another.&lt;SEP&gt;Speech translation is an artificial intelligence and computational linguistics domain focused on translating spoken audio into text or audio in another language.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c10d3bd2a312359e6f2e1f854a8d2ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Morphologically Rich Languages">
      <data key="d0">Morphologically Rich Languages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Languages characterized by complex word formation rules that present distinct translation challenges, especially when translating without English as an intermediary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Africa">
      <data key="d0">Africa</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A continent whose indigenous languages are predominantly low-resourced and historically underrepresented in machine translation benchmarks.&lt;SEP&gt;A continent with over 2,000 languages, many of which are complex and low-resourced in terms of machine translation datasets.&lt;SEP&gt;A geographic region whose native languages represent low-resource language pairs that receive insufficient focus in MT research.&lt;SEP&gt;Africa is a continent analyzed for language representation where corpora show consistent agreement for areas with available ground-truth data.&lt;SEP&gt;Africa is a continent identified as a potential future region for closer digital and linguistic communication among related languages.&lt;SEP&gt;Africa is a continent where countries show lower web-crawled data production due to lower internet access, yet many show over-representation in Twitter data.&lt;SEP&gt;Africa is a continent whose indigenous languages face challenges in digital resources, research discoverability, and machine translation development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d7d53ab63937232bbcc4be9c7080dd31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a214a03aad6b4356ff5b17786166ad02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-927600095f9514bfcdfdefa43cf8a474&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f50b15c876beec2eae8bf6437e8409aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d721fe5dad0029252753a2c12969e26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe3fb6b107774e12d36226865853669d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="South America">
      <data key="d0">South America</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographic region containing low-resource language pairs that are underrepresented in mainstream MT research.&lt;SEP&gt;South America is a continent where both Twitter and web-crawled corpora align in language use predictions.&lt;SEP&gt;South America is a region identified as being over-represented in Twitter data relative to population size.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d721fe5dad0029252753a2c12969e26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7d53ab63937232bbcc4be9c7080dd31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Europe">
      <data key="d0">Europe</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographic region referenced through the 13 European languages represented in the labeled Twitter dataset.&lt;SEP&gt;A geographic region whose languages, alongside English, have historically been the excessive focus of MT research and development.&lt;SEP&gt;A geographical region whose closely related languages were studied in previous character-level statistical machine translation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Europe is a continent and region with substantial communication among people speaking related languages.&lt;SEP&gt;Europe is a geographic region where web-crawled and Twitter corpora demonstrate agreement in predicted language usage against census ground-truth.&lt;SEP&gt;Europe is the geographic region encompassing the thirteen native languages from which tweet corpora were sampled and analyzed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a214a03aad6b4356ff5b17786166ad02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7d53ab63937232bbcc4be9c7080dd31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c672772b311a70f628b5b6b878eb3f37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8756bb2d18f7993da0c11080bc5f15a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f598ed7171b8214c363358a6167512f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffaa7abbd8709e94a9523e6b922ac96b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bar-Hillel">
      <data key="d0">Bar-Hillel</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Bar-Hillel was an early machine translation researcher who asserted in 1960 that machine translation systems require specific knowledge to resolve word ambiguity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carpuat">
      <data key="d0">Carpuat</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Carpuat is a researcher whose contributions significantly lessened the word ambiguity problem in machine translation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Moussallem">
      <data key="d0">Moussallem</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who, along with colleagues, developed THOTH, an approach for translating and enriching knowledge graphs across languages.&lt;SEP&gt;Moussallem is a researcher who integrated multilingual entity linking algorithms and knowledge graph embeddings into neural machine translation models, and also released a multilingual linked idioms dataset.&lt;SEP&gt;Moussallem is a researcher who studied the limitations of domain-specific language models when translating ambiguous or multi-domain text in machine translation systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3bf41e0b7ea425266139faf090ad226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Koehn">
      <data key="d0">Koehn</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Koehn is a researcher who identified named entities as a difficult and shared problem across machine translation and semantic web fields.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Zealand">
      <data key="d0">New Zealand</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">New Zealand is a country identified as the geographical origin of the described Sauvignon Blanc wine.&lt;SEP&gt;New Zealand is an island country where the term Kiwi originates as a family name rooted in Māori culture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ccf1d827990dd6a5adc3c9f90a8f419</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DBpedia">
      <data key="d0">DBpedia</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A crowd-sourced community project and structured knowledge base that extracts data from Wikipedia, utilized for cross-lingual knowledge graph tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;A structured knowledge base and community project extracting information from Wikipedia, used as a knowledge source and infobox property repository for training the QA model.&lt;SEP&gt;DBpedia is a community and knowledge repository project that extracts structured infobox properties and n-triples from Wikipedia for training models.&lt;SEP&gt;DBpedia is a community project and open-domain knowledge base repository that extracts structured factual data from Wikipedia.&lt;SEP&gt;DBpedia is a community project that extracts structured knowledge and n-triples from the Wikipedia repository to enable semantic querying.&lt;SEP&gt;DBpedia is an open knowledge base project that extracts structured semantic information and triples from Wikipedia.&lt;SEP&gt;DBpedia is an open-knowledge base and Semantic Web resource accessible via SPARQL queries to support entity disambiguation.&lt;SEP&gt;The project and organization maintaining the structured knowledge base from which resources were selected for the verbalization experiment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb73055a3a263fcef16382513853f022&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3bf41e0b7ea425266139faf090ad226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0cfc66c51b34132fd3046873eac6d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea9c29cc7a5d0f200ae552bfc4494ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fff775482c4d8a3088b428b10d9f761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Word Sense Disambiguation">
      <data key="d0">Word Sense Disambiguation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core natural language processing task aimed at identifying the correct meaning or sense of an ambiguous word within a specific context.&lt;SEP&gt;A core natural language processing task focused on computationally identifying the correct meaning of an ambiguous word within a specific context.&lt;SEP&gt;Word Sense Disambiguation (WSD) is a computational linguistics task focused on identifying the correct meaning or sense of an ambiguous word in context.&lt;SEP&gt;Word Sense Disambiguation (WSD) is a natural language processing task that identifies the appropriate meaning of a word in context.&lt;SEP&gt;Word Sense Disambiguation (WSD) is a task and subfield in natural language processing focused on identifying the correct meaning or sense of an ambiguous word in context.&lt;SEP&gt;Word Sense Disambiguation is a natural language processing task aimed at identifying the intended meaning and correct target translation of polysemous and homonymous words.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626602288681ee562ad75771b687c6be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2a718261409ed817eb2c8548c15249f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Named Entity Recognition and Disambiguation">
      <data key="d0">Named Entity Recognition and Disambiguation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Named Entity Recognition and Disambiguation is a computational task that identifies proper names in text and links them to knowledge base entries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topic Modelling">
      <data key="d0">Topic Modelling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A text processing category of techniques suggested as a potential method to alleviate the influence of high-frequency words across different thematic contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;An advanced text-mining method proposed as future work to improve user matching and account tracking across social networks.&lt;SEP&gt;Topic modelling is a text-processing technique proposed to analyze context across small segments of source text prior to translation to guide disambiguation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c18ec95758198fc24e4adbe977b2a821&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9539a4c4fcbd235bc0ccbc8f3fd26a81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Syntactic Disambiguation">
      <data key="d0">Syntactic Disambiguation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Syntactic disambiguation is the challenge of identifying the correct grammatical structure and verbal tense of sentences in natural language processing and translation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Semantic Disambiguation">
      <data key="d0">Semantic Disambiguation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Semantic disambiguation is the process of distinguishing between the different senses of polysemous and homonymous words within a specific context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ontology">
      <data key="d0">Ontology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A formal conceptualization of a knowledge domain defining classes, subclasses, and relations between entities, commonly specified using OWL.&lt;SEP&gt;An ontology is a formal representation of knowledge and relations among concepts and properties, used within Semantic Web frameworks to support translation and reasoning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa0a1d40aed971d10ad0f0f2c4938cf4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SPARQL">
      <data key="d0">SPARQL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A semantic query language designed to retrieve and manipulate data stored in RDF format.&lt;SEP&gt;A semantic query language used to retrieve and manipulate data stored in Resource Description Framework (RDF) triple format within Linked Data.&lt;SEP&gt;SPARQL is a semantic query language designed to retrieve and manipulate data stored in Resource Description Framework (RDF) format and Semantic Web knowledge bases.&lt;SEP&gt;SPARQL is a semantic query language designed to retrieve and manipulate data stored in Resource Description Framework (RDF) format.&lt;SEP&gt;SPARQL is a standard query language and protocol designed for querying Semantic Web data stored in RDF format.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb73055a3a263fcef16382513853f022&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-927600095f9514bfcdfdefa43cf8a474&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa79759ff02333ae3d3a1d802134a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reasoners">
      <data key="d0">Reasoners</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Reasoners are algorithmic inference engines used in the Semantic Web to deduce logical relationships and rules, notable for run-time performance limitations in real-time MT.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pre-Editing">
      <data key="d0">Pre-Editing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pre-editing is a translation workflow technique where ambiguous words are identified and annotated in the source text prior to running machine translation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Post-Editing">
      <data key="d0">Post-Editing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Post-editing is a translation workflow technique where Semantic Web tools or human reviewers refine and correct the target language translation output.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Named Entities">
      <data key="d0">Named Entities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Named entities are proper nouns, names, and cultural references that often overlap with common nouns across languages and cause translation errors.&lt;SEP&gt;Named entities are real-world entities identified in natural language texts, frequently appearing as informal name variants in social media posts.&lt;SEP&gt;Named entities are structured real-world referents, such as persons or locations, whose identification is challenged by informal variations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-46a782dfec4ed85a41291a31e4dcf6cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4752470c8ccb777bad45ef52fde0243d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="C. Shi">
      <data key="d0">C. Shi</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">C. Shi is a researcher whose work encoded types of entities alongside words to improve Chinese-to-English machine translation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="J. P. McCrae">
      <data key="d0">J. P. McCrae</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">J. P. McCrae is a researcher who conducted work on ontology localisation by translating terms in a monolingual ontology into a bilingual ontology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ristoski">
      <data key="d0">Ristoski</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Ristoski is a researcher who developed bilingual embeddings using RDF based on Word2vec algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Arčan">
      <data key="d0">Arčan</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who, together with Buitelaar, presented an approach to translate domain-specific expressions represented by English knowledge bases to make them accessible for other languages.&lt;SEP&gt;Arčan is a researcher who developed an approach to translate domain-specific expressions represented by English knowledge bases to other languages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3bf41e0b7ea425266139faf090ad226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Buitelaar">
      <data key="d0">Buitelaar</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who co-authored work on translating domain-specific knowledge bases into German to assist in machine translation.&lt;SEP&gt;Buitelaar is a researcher who, alongside Arčan, worked on translating domain-specific English knowledge bases for medical and financial domains to German.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3bf41e0b7ea425266139faf090ad226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Google">
      <data key="d0">Google</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A global technology company that developed the Google Home smart agent.&lt;SEP&gt;A multinational technology company whose Google News corpus containing over 100 billion words was used to pre-train Word2Vec word embeddings.&lt;SEP&gt;A multinational technology company whose Google News dataset was used to train the word2vec model.&lt;SEP&gt;A multinational technology company whose Google Translate tool and TensorFlow Research Cloud (TFRC) supported the research.&lt;SEP&gt;A multinational technology company whose translation tool, Google Translate, supports a subset of official South African languages.&lt;SEP&gt;A technology company mentioned as an example entity attribute within the DynoNet knowledge graph evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;A technology company that created the analogy dataset used to evaluate semantic and syntactic regularity in vector embedding spaces.&lt;SEP&gt;A technology company whose Google News corpus provided pre-trained Word2Vec embeddings for BiLSTM models.&lt;SEP&gt;Google is a global technology company that developed the Google News corpus used to train the word2vec embedding model.&lt;SEP&gt;Google is a global technology company that provided research funding and computing support for the work.&lt;SEP&gt;Google is a multinational technology company that developed the Inception-v3 neural network architecture used as an experimental baseline.&lt;SEP&gt;Google is a technology company associated with the Google analogy task mentioned in the context of evaluation results.&lt;SEP&gt;Google is a technology company that created the Inception-v3 deep learning architecture used for multimodal and image-based attribute extraction baselines.&lt;SEP&gt;Google is a technology company that deploys statistical machine translation systems with recognition patterns for non-standard speech.&lt;SEP&gt;Google is a technology company whose Tensor Processing Unit (TPU) hardware was utilized to train Transformer neural translation models.&lt;SEP&gt;Google is a technology company whose analogy dataset is used to evaluate the semantic and syntactic structure of word embedding models.&lt;SEP&gt;Google is a technology company whose pre-trained model parameters on Chinese general corpus were used in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Google is a technology company whose pre-trained word2vec models and Google Translator were leveraged to develop lexicon and embedding features for text classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Google is a technology company whose translation tool, Google Translate, is used for translating extracted Tamil sentences to English for qualitative analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f7cfb64157b0b1becf1f72e036758aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d061db4b0ef06b5f3c1d715a602dc1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddef736560bcb899db81b75205f4df60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a214a03aad6b4356ff5b17786166ad02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-927600095f9514bfcdfdefa43cf8a474&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2af0dac5bc5afdea73673fd2462c1e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-784f828261b96cc3c6df35aff5b1cae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aa01d1737dbc0f2a321f7d1e6e0289d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f50b15c876beec2eae8bf6437e8409aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289c3be854417f1af5a604180e6310cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152eae14a34b247b0c7628803502e787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18770431e1f17cd9efc7aedcdb98d303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd1654ff6fd1aefc2ac7a0fb3a18a4eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ad8b9a3ff673a8919c974c24205949f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af2c44c466ad216bc8f3bec17868b71f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9645cad08b5b060013cac117d156f888</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bing">
      <data key="d0">Bing</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Bing is a search engine and service developed by Microsoft that utilizes statistical machine translation systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wikipedia">
      <data key="d0">Wikipedia</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A free multilingual online encyclopedia and collaborative knowledge base used as an external resource for translation.&lt;SEP&gt;A free online encyclopedia cited as an external source of raw text entries and world facts for dialogue models.&lt;SEP&gt;A free online encyclopedia whose text corpus was utilized to train both fastText and the Baroni semantic embedding models.&lt;SEP&gt;A multilingual online encyclopedia whose corpus is commonly used for evaluating language and word embedding models.&lt;SEP&gt;An online collaborative encyclopedia whose textual data is analyzed and noted to contain contextual gender bias in datasets like WikiGenderBias.&lt;SEP&gt;An online encyclopedia whose text corpora served as the training resource for the monolingual word embeddings used in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;An online multilingual encyclopedia used as a data source for extracting entity articles and gender information to build the WikiGenderBias dataset.&lt;SEP&gt;An open-collaboration online encyclopedia whose texts are noted to contain latent context bias affecting NLP models.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is a collaborative online encyclopedia and knowledge repository whose information serves as the basis for the DBpedia project.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is a collaborative online encyclopedia whose 2006 cleaned dump was utilized to create the Text8 corpus for model training.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is a collaborative open online encyclopedia used as a foundational knowledge source for data extraction and question answering systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is a free online encyclopedia whose articles provide the reading passages for the SQuAD machine comprehension benchmark.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is a multilingual online collaborative encyclopedia referenced as a rich source of comparable corpora for parallel sentence mining.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is a multilingual online encyclopedia providing comparable corpus articles utilized to extract parallel sentence pairs and structured knowledge.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is a multilingual online encyclopedia whose bilingual articles and dumps are used to extract parallel sentences for machine translation.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is a multilingual online encyclopedia whose language editions provide content and semantic structure used in machine translation and DBpedia.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is an online collaborative encyclopedia whose articles and infoboxes were analyzed for gender bias discrepancies.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is an open multilingual online encyclopedia whose comparable multilingual articles were used to extract parallel sentences.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is an open online encyclopedia whose biographical articles were analyzed for life events and gender bias.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is an open online encyclopedia whose text corpus was used to train the fastText word embeddings evaluated in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is an open online encyclopedia whose text entries are used as a factual knowledge base to ground dialogue system utterances.&lt;SEP&gt;Wikipedia is an open-content online encyclopedia utilized as a source of non-official formal text for training language identification models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb73055a3a263fcef16382513853f022&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b31134945186e62e7effccb3ba89b180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdef2f67b0ab332d8da79c41fb0e58c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b470fa40233ba7e36a9f7c70bafce9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2af0dac5bc5afdea73673fd2462c1e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aa01d1737dbc0f2a321f7d1e6e0289d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-116526d304daa442dbc1df519e7f8fc5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-362346ad5b3a18dced3a68b0e282ea62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a857415cf32d5d04b955b0da8d697209&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f7cfb64157b0b1becf1f72e036758aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa612722a8775613a3ecb4a189613e3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc1405d1609fdbebb00fe9dd18235f8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fff775482c4d8a3088b428b10d9f761&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef7b95a2f35faad97942f87543b7ab1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3bf41e0b7ea425266139faf090ad226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ca3ab8f8c0dca0a40d79a8ee4226255&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-275b54efb44b71f0c8102cd8da9ac971&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea9c29cc7a5d0f200ae552bfc4494ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-806c5b143029efcdf6de258a039af615&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b32cb050de56f58b5792720c6bc3e42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4117dfdbcf8427f9aa5d8d43444fb707</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Russia">
      <data key="d0">Russia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A country referenced in connection with online trolls identified on Twitter.&lt;SEP&gt;A country whose Russian language dataset was analyzed in comparison with annotator agreement.&lt;SEP&gt;Russia is a country exhibiting false-positive language occurrences in both web-crawled and Twitter datasets.&lt;SEP&gt;Russia is a country referenced as an example geographic location of a user reading foreign language web content.&lt;SEP&gt;Russia is a nation mentioned as an illustrative example of the nationality-adjective morphological transformation rule.&lt;SEP&gt;Russia is identified as being over-represented in Twitter data relative to its population size.&lt;SEP&gt;Russia is the country associated with the state-linked Internet Research Agency (IRA) troll accounts that mimicked US users.&lt;SEP&gt;Russia is the country of origin for the Internet Research Agency and its coordinated troll operations on social media.&lt;SEP&gt;Russia is the country where the IRA social media influence campaign originated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f30e651d33c86668592dd08985152d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e9d6cd8b1a1f2b1ece006c42d145e4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-075e25354814630ae73f42287e577734&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7d53ab63937232bbcc4be9c7080dd31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd39034576eb8de5d243305db77f4b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d721fe5dad0029252753a2c12969e26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be334478ba316675f89bc24ce6e3c03a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56672a5cb0c14c3d38c148dbe5205622</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WMT English-German Translation Task">
      <data key="d0">WMT English-German Translation Task</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The WMT English-German translation task is a shared machine translation evaluation benchmark on which entity linking models achieved significant evaluation metric improvements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Standard Speech">
      <data key="d0">Non-Standard Speech</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-standard speech is a linguistic category encompassing colloquial expressions, slang, multi-word expressions, abbreviations, and idioms that challenge MT systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Entity Linking">
      <data key="d0">Entity Linking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A natural language processing task that identifies and links named entity mentions in text to corresponding nodes in a knowledge base.&lt;SEP&gt;Entity Linking is a technique that identifies entity mentions in text and connects them to corresponding entries in reference knowledge bases or graphs.&lt;SEP&gt;Entity linking is a subfield of information processing and knowledge representation concerned with connecting named mentions to specific entities in a knowledge graph.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3bf41e0b7ea425266139faf090ad226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-261a6f8ccfb17ebb9be72e74900d0a04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Knowledge Graph Embeddings">
      <data key="d0">Knowledge Graph Embeddings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Knowledge Graph Embeddings are vector representations of entities and relations from a knowledge graph, used in neural models to preserve semantic structure and contextual information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RDF">
      <data key="d0">RDF</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A foundational framework and data specification for representing information in the Semantic Web.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource Description Framework (RDF) is a key foundational Semantic Web data representation language and standard data model.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource Description Framework (RDF) is a standard data model and language of the Semantic Web used to represent information and triples in knowledge bases.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource Description Framework, a foundational semantic web standard used for representing structured data via subject-predicate-object triples.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource Description Framework, a standard model for data interchange and knowledge representation structured as subject-predicate-object triples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-927600095f9514bfcdfdefa43cf8a474&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa79759ff02333ae3d3a1d802134a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fcb14d19cf40f8290f78e99be61a949&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa0a1d40aed971d10ad0f0f2c4938cf4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOAF">
      <data key="d0">FOAF</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Friend of a Friend (FOAF) is a Semantic Web ontology and vocabulary that describes persons, their activities, interests, and relations to other people and objects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIOC">
      <data key="d0">SIOC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Semantically-Interlinked Online Communities (SIOC) is an ontology used to describe online community information, including topics and user forum interactions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Word2vec">
      <data key="d0">Word2vec</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Word2vec is a family of model architectures used to generate word and concept vector representations based on linguistic contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Word2vec is a foundational family of model architectures and software implementations used to learn distributed vector representations of words.&lt;SEP&gt;Word2vec is a natural language processing model and tool used to learn continuous vector representations of words from Twitter data.&lt;SEP&gt;Word2vec is a neural word embedding approach used to generate vector representations carrying semantic meaning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f3e8fa58e562bb861dae62114ff9c810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf3bb0dbc6eb81bded1d21569d42693f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b5684eaac7a3f52271bc1ff14d5ff1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Out-of-Vocabulary">
      <data key="d0">Out-of-Vocabulary</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Out-of-Vocabulary (OOV) refers to words, names, or phrases encountered during inference that were not present in the system's training dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multi-Word Expressions">
      <data key="d0">Multi-Word Expressions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Multi-Word Expressions (MWE) are lexical items composed of multiple words whose collective meaning cannot always be inferred directly from the individual words.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Evaluation Metrics">
      <data key="d0">Evaluation Metrics</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Evaluation Metrics in machine translation, including BLEU, METEOR, and CHRF3, quantify the lexical and semantic quality of generated translations against reference texts.&lt;SEP&gt;Evaluation metrics are performance criteria used to score classification systems, including macroaveraged recall, positive and negative averaged F1 score, and accuracy.&lt;SEP&gt;Evaluation metrics represent quantitative and qualitative criteria—such as BLEU, perplexity, and embedding-based measures—used to evaluate dialogue systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Evaluation metrics, including BLEU, METEOR, and chrF, are quantitative evaluation methods used to assess the quality and correctness of generated text against gold-standard references.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b5684eaac7a3f52271bc1ff14d5ff1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb3ab7fc50f538bef2c8ddb57a8a6b95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea9c29cc7a5d0f200ae552bfc4494ad2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Newstest Datasets">
      <data key="d0">Newstest Datasets</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Newstest Datasets (2014–2018) are benchmark evaluation sets used in the annual WMT shared translation tasks to assess translation model performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a21c67281db092efd7427a6f05d8ea5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="German Federal Ministry of Transport and Digital Infrastructure">
      <data key="d0">German Federal Ministry of Transport and Digital Infrastructure</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A German federal agency (BMVI) that funded research under the LIMBO and OPAL projects.&lt;SEP&gt;A German federal ministry (BMVI) that funded semantic web research through the LIMBO and OPAL projects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-927600095f9514bfcdfdefa43cf8a474&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3bf41e0b7ea425266139faf090ad226</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brazilian National Council for Scientific and Technological Development">
      <data key="d0">Brazilian National Council for Scientific and Technological Development</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Brazilian government agency (CNPq) dedicated to the promotion of scientific and technological research, which supported the research.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3bf41e0b7ea425266139faf090ad226</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Knowledge Graphs">
      <data key="d0">Knowledge Graphs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A structural representation of entities and relations used to augment conversational agents with factual and contextual common sense.&lt;SEP&gt;Structured representations of real-world knowledge used in multilingual tasks, entity linking, and fact checking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3bf41e0b7ea425266139faf090ad226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f7cfb64157b0b1becf1f72e036758aa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Compression">
      <data key="d0">Model Compression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A broad field of techniques aimed at reducing the parameter size, memory footprint, and latency of deep learning models.&lt;SEP&gt;A category of machine learning techniques, such as weight pruning, matrix factorization, and knowledge distillation, designed to reduce model size and latency.&lt;SEP&gt;A category of techniques in machine learning aimed at reducing model size, memory footprint, and computational overhead while retaining performance.&lt;SEP&gt;A collection of deep learning techniques designed to reduce memory consumption and computational cost of neural networks, particularly in low-resource environments.&lt;SEP&gt;A machine learning paradigm aimed at reducing model parameter sizes and latency for memory-intensive or latency-critical deployment scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;Model compression refers to techniques used to reduce the size, memory footprint, and latency of deep learning models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3bf41e0b7ea425266139faf090ad226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1f1d3da436f9f4b7eadca13b0e5803e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ff808f5f84391609fd609fe291c7f4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7655d654b459dfdba7d9c1f562898a07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69bdb2043fb72d1301a9087fb8f43b95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4f9917b54f845e14e0e4edafd46cb2d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Knowledge Distillation">
      <data key="d0">Knowledge Distillation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A compression technique that transfers knowledge from a large teacher model to a smaller student model by minimizing loss functions.&lt;SEP&gt;A compression technique where a compact student model learns to replicate the behaviors and representations of a larger teacher model.&lt;SEP&gt;A machine learning technique where a compact student model is trained to mimic the behaviors and representations of a larger teacher model.&lt;SEP&gt;A model compression technique where a compact student model is trained to approximate the function of a larger teacher model.&lt;SEP&gt;A model compression technique where a compact student model is trained to reproduce the behavior and representations of a larger teacher model.&lt;SEP&gt;A model compression technique where a smaller, compact student model is trained to approximate the representations and functions of a larger model.&lt;SEP&gt;Knowledge distillation is a compression and training technique where a compact student model learns to reproduce the behavior of a larger teacher model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3bf41e0b7ea425266139faf090ad226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1f1d3da436f9f4b7eadca13b0e5803e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ff808f5f84391609fd609fe291c7f4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7655d654b459dfdba7d9c1f562898a07&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69bdb2043fb72d1301a9087fb8f43b95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56a06d096f62c8282f59e57d190e7d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4f9917b54f845e14e0e4edafd46cb2d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germany">
      <data key="d0">Germany</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A European country whose German language tweet dataset was evaluated across multiple annotators.&lt;SEP&gt;A European country whose parliamentary proceedings have been analyzed in computational political science and ideology prediction studies.&lt;SEP&gt;A country in Central Europe whose language, institutions, and government ministry are referenced in the context of translation research and funding.&lt;SEP&gt;Germany is a European country whose obituary traditions, visual elements, and textual norms were examined in comparative cultural research.&lt;SEP&gt;Germany is a country whose obituaries were analyzed by Barth2013 for visual and textual elements.&lt;SEP&gt;Germany is the European nation associated with the German language texts, corpora, and transcripts gathered from public domain sources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3bf41e0b7ea425266139faf090ad226&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e9d6cd8b1a1f2b1ece006c42d145e4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a42d2500d95a11dc6c778a73c5dba9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc1405d1609fdbebb00fe9dd18235f8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-707d9bb0e2896feb599f211f8cc655a0</data>
    </node>
