    <node id="Kocabiyikoglu">
      <data key="d0">Kocabiyikoglu</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher whose audiobook-based corpus creation approach is mirrored in the LibriVoxDeEn methodology.&lt;SEP&gt;Kocabiyikoglu is a researcher credited with establishing a 5-point evaluation scale for assessing text alignment quality.&lt;SEP&gt;Kocabiyikoglu is a researcher whose prior work defined evaluation scales and benchmarks for text and audio alignment quality in speech translation corpora.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c10d3bd2a312359e6f2e1f854a8d2ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Speech Recognition">
      <data key="d0">Speech Recognition</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A computational category focused on automatically transcribing spoken language audio into text.&lt;SEP&gt;A technology and field of NLP enabling the recognition and translation of spoken language into text, supported by neural language models.&lt;SEP&gt;An NLP and signal processing task where transfer learning methods have previously been successfully applied.&lt;SEP&gt;An automated technological process of converting spoken language into text, which often omits punctuation and adds complexity to intent parsing.&lt;SEP&gt;Speech recognition is a domain in artificial intelligence and computer science focused on the translation of spoken language into text.&lt;SEP&gt;Speech recognition is a domain of computing and artificial intelligence focused on transcribing spoken language into text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b98e14081bd42f130f547ac6df8114f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c10d3bd2a312359e6f2e1f854a8d2ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18770431e1f17cd9efc7aedcdb98d303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fff775482c4d8a3088b428b10d9f761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="End-to-End Speech Translation">
      <data key="d0">End-to-End Speech Translation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A direct speech translation approach utilizing a single sequence-to-sequence model to convert speech audio directly to text in a target language without an intermediate text transcription stage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0c10d3bd2a312359e6f2e1f854a8d2ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Text Translation">
      <data key="d0">Text Translation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The computational process of converting written text from a source language into a target language, often combined with speech recognition in cascaded translation pipelines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0c10d3bd2a312359e6f2e1f854a8d2ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multi-Task Learning">
      <data key="d0">Multi-Task Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A machine learning paradigm that jointly trains models across multiple related tasks to share representations, extract common features, and improve generalization performance.&lt;SEP&gt;A machine learning paradigm where multiple related learning tasks are solved simultaneously by sharing representations and sub-networks.&lt;SEP&gt;A machine learning paradigm where multiple tasks are learned simultaneously in parallel to share representations and improve performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Multi-task learning is a machine learning paradigm in which multiple related tasks are learned jointly to leverage shared representations and improve generalization performance across tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;Multi-task learning is a machine learning paradigm that trains models across multiple related tasks concurrently to exploit shared feature representations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-99df0a2940e224839126173e8c2bc846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-784f828261b96cc3c6df35aff5b1cae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c10d3bd2a312359e6f2e1f854a8d2ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d95ef3b7585c8c501112f173f330c95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f5016d3aa81277cabc5c947f1ca64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Audiobooks">
      <data key="d0">Audiobooks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Spoken voice recordings of written book texts, often serving as a primary source for creating speech recognition and speech translation corpora.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0c10d3bd2a312359e6f2e1f854a8d2ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Read Speech">
      <data key="d0">Read Speech</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of recorded speech characterized by low disfluencies due to speakers reading directly from prepared texts or books.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0c10d3bd2a312359e6f2e1f854a8d2ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Speech Alignment">
      <data key="d0">Speech Alignment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of synchronizing spoken audio segments with their corresponding textual transcriptions using forced alignment algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0c10d3bd2a312359e6f2e1f854a8d2ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentence Alignment">
      <data key="d0">Sentence Alignment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sentence alignment is a natural language processing task and technique aimed at identifying corresponding sentence pairs across bilingual or multilingual texts.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of matching parallel sentence pairs between source and target language texts for translation corpus construction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fff775482c4d8a3088b428b10d9f761&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c10d3bd2a312359e6f2e1f854a8d2ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Word Embeddings">
      <data key="d0">Word Embeddings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dense continuous vector representations of words used in neural language models and natural language processing tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;Dense semantic vector representations used as inputs to the BiLSTM network for obituary segmentation.&lt;SEP&gt;Dense vector representations of words in a vocabulary where each column represents a word and rows represent latent features, used in neural networks and NLP models.&lt;SEP&gt;Dense vector representations of words that capture semantic meaning, used to bootstrap neural language models.&lt;SEP&gt;Vector representations of words capturing lexical and semantic relationships, evaluated across natural language processing tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;Vector representations of words in natural language processing, constructed through various architectures including character n-grams, CNNs, and attention mechanisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Vector representations of words in the neural network that are transferred from the parent model to initialize the child model's vocabulary.&lt;SEP&gt;Vector space representations of words that capture lexical and character-level information, commonly used in natural language processing and bilingual lexicon induction.&lt;SEP&gt;Vector space representations of words used as inputs for neural language models.&lt;SEP&gt;Word Embeddings are dense semantic vector representations of words used to capture semantic relationships in natural language processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Word Embeddings are dense vector representations of words in continuous vector spaces that capture semantic relationships, often enriched with domain-specific data.&lt;SEP&gt;Word Embeddings are vector representations of text tokens projected into a common dimensionality alongside queries.&lt;SEP&gt;Word embeddings are a distributional semantics approach that represents words as continuous real-valued vectors based on semantic and syntactic properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Word embeddings are dense vector representations of text capturing semantic, syntactic, and sentiment information of words and phrases.&lt;SEP&gt;Word embeddings are dense vector representations of textual vocabulary that can encode gender associations and are subject to debiasing techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Word embeddings are dense vector representations of words capturing syntactic and semantic properties in natural language processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Word embeddings are dense, low-dimensional vector representations of words learned from large unlabeled corpora via unsupervised neural networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb73055a3a263fcef16382513853f022&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bac16ae7b48f68b81dcf01699b27c93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b5684eaac7a3f52271bc1ff14d5ff1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e759311c40b86a32804c73d3e9508819&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7818b9847aa576cb0d1559c6c5e963ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae1796dede39736a6ce61ec4cb1e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0f9c89ffb57d88b6a05fbcd7d909d7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626602288681ee562ad75771b687c6be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c10d3bd2a312359e6f2e1f854a8d2ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289c3be854417f1af5a604180e6310cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9896f0582e2237c4a0dd8169f6817110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3e8fa58e562bb861dae62114ff9c810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be334478ba316675f89bc24ce6e3c03a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6c6ba3246066dafb17ed3cbf673534&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37ae86766195404daaa0c376964357ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47f43d3218257ab88ccde621eadece2c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TED Talks">
      <data key="d0">TED Talks</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Public presentations recorded and distributed by TED, widely used as a spoken language benchmark and training corpus across multiple languages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0c10d3bd2a312359e6f2e1f854a8d2ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Archive.org">
      <data key="d0">Archive.org</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Archive.org (the Internet Archive) is a digital library organization providing free access to public domain web pages and materials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heidelberg University">
      <data key="d0">Heidelberg University</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Heidelberg University is an academic research institution hosting the Statistical NLP Group, which maintains the German-English LibriVox speech corpus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Berard">
      <data key="d0">Berard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Berard is a researcher cited for conducting speech translation experiments on the Augmented LibriSpeech corpus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Text Alignment">
      <data key="d0">Text Alignment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Text alignment is a computational linguistics task involving the pairing and synchronization of parallel text segments across languages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Preprocessing">
      <data key="d0">Data Preprocessing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Data preprocessing is a category of data preparation procedures involving noise removal, audio trimming, and sentence segmentation for machine learning datasets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Zeno.org">
      <data key="d0">Zeno.org</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Zeno.org is an online public domain digital library used as a source for crawling German public domain literature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spiegel Gutenberg">
      <data key="d0">Spiegel Gutenberg</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Spiegel Gutenberg (gutenberg.spiegel.de) is a German public domain literary archive and digital portal used for crawling text transcripts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Statistical NLP Group">
      <data key="d0">Statistical NLP Group</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Statistical NLP Group is a research group within Heidelberg University that developed, evaluated, and hosted the German-English LibriVox dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentence Segmentation">
      <data key="d0">Sentence Segmentation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sentence segmentation is a natural language processing task that involves splitting running text into discrete sentence units.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Text-to-Speech Alignment">
      <data key="d0">Text-to-Speech Alignment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Text-to-speech alignment is the process of synchronizing written text sentences with corresponding start and end timestamps in spoken audio recordings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Automatic Speech Recognition">
      <data key="d0">Automatic Speech Recognition</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An application task that uses neural language models for output rescoring to improve accuracy.&lt;SEP&gt;Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) is a speech processing and NLP application that utilizes subword units for modeling speech.&lt;SEP&gt;Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) is a speech technology category enabling computer systems to transcribe human speech into text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3e6c6ba3246066dafb17ed3cbf673534&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e060c5bbcfa40d71aa9fb0a2e63bff30</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dynamic Time Warping">
      <data key="d0">Dynamic Time Warping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dynamic Time Warping (DTW) is an algorithm used in time-series analysis to align audio representations synthesized from text with real audio.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Human Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A qualitative study involving human annotators and crowd workers to rate and rank generated sentences based on content fidelity, style preservation, and fluency.&lt;SEP&gt;An empirical assessment process wherein human annotators score and rank generated sentences based on content fidelity, style preservation, and fluency.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Evaluation is a structured assessment event where annotators evaluated text alignment accuracy on a 5-point scale across confidence score bins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fa216244e9956f33ee1e83a4b072080&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08e25f0ce4d7d5635eb6aed1b2807f91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WikiMatrix">
      <data key="d0">WikiMatrix</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">WikiMatrix is a parallel multilingual text corpus mined from Wikipedia used to generate custom sentence-alignment dictionaries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TIGER Corpus">
      <data key="d0">TIGER Corpus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TIGER Corpus is a German language corpus utilized to train and support German sentence segmentation models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WikiNER Dataset">
      <data key="d0">WikiNER Dataset</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">WikiNER is an annotated entity dataset used alongside the TIGER corpus for training German sentence segmentation models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Augmented LibriSpeech">
      <data key="d0">Augmented LibriSpeech</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Augmented LibriSpeech is a benchmark speech translation corpus used as a reference point for comparing corpus size and translation quality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-481c2184bc4195d808fe5fe2cc4f084c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="German Research Foundation">
      <data key="d0">German Research Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The German Research Foundation (DFG) is a major research funding organization in Germany that supported the speech translation corpus research under grant RI-2221/4-1.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Krippendorff">
      <data key="d0">Krippendorff</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Klaus Krippendorff is a social scientist and statistician known for developing Krippendorff's alpha-reliability score used to calculate inter-annotator agreement.&lt;SEP&gt;Krippendorff is an academic and methodologist known for developing Krippendorff's Alpha-reliability, a generalized agreement metric used for inter-rater reliability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f37521f7c9c4230a9fe0c9f8b7f1604</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Charleston">
      <data key="d0">Charleston</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Charleston is a geographic port city referenced in one of the aligned bilingual literary translation examples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adversarial Examples">
      <data key="d0">Adversarial Examples</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adversarial examples are inputs designed with subtle perturbations to fool machine learning and text classification models.&lt;SEP&gt;Adversarial examples represent a category of perturbed inputs generated by applying perturbation vectors to evaluate model robustness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67aef3e225567bceaa8ddbf5e9866527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ottilie">
      <data key="d0">Ottilie</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Ottilie is a literary figure featured in the aligned corpus evaluation examples who is depicted in devotion to Edward and caring for a child.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eduard">
      <data key="d0">Eduard</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Eduard (Edward) is a literary character appearing in the German-English translation alignment examples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Charlotte">
      <data key="d0">Charlotte</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Charlotte is a character in the parallel text alignment examples who ascends a cliff alongside Ottilie.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Schigolch">
      <data key="d0">Schigolch</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Schigolch is a dramatic character featured in the bilingual text alignment excerpt dreaming of Christmas pudding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lulu">
      <data key="d0">Lulu</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Lulu is a dramatic character referenced in the text alignment examples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lord Henry">
      <data key="d0">Lord Henry</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Lord Henry is a literary character from the bilingual corpus examples who murmurs 'Fin de siecle'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Folker">
      <data key="d0">Folker</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Folker is a heroic character cited in the German-English alignment samples as a companion and banner bearer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kakambo">
      <data key="d0">Kakambo</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Kakambo (Cacambo) is a character appearing in the alignment corpus samples who never lost his head.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Corpus Alignment Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Corpus Alignment Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The manual evaluation experiment where two annotators assessed audio-text and text-text alignment quality across multiple bins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Audio-Text Alignment">
      <data key="d0">Audio-Text Alignment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Audio-text alignment is the process and task of pairing spoken audio intervals with their exact textual transcriptions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Text-To-Text Sentence Alignment">
      <data key="d0">Text-To-Text Sentence Alignment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Text-to-text sentence alignment is the computational and linguistic task of pairing corresponding source sentences with target language translations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Black Box Attack">
      <data key="d0">Black Box Attack</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A class of adversarial machine learning attacks that generates perturbations against models without requiring knowledge of their internal parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4524baad525e91d77888428268a1920e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BAE">
      <data key="d0">BAE</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BAE (BERT-based Adversarial Examples) is a black-box adversarial attack framework that generates grammatically correct and semantically coherent adversarial text perturbations using masked language models.&lt;SEP&gt;BERT-based Adversarial Examples (BAE) is an adversarial attack methodology for text classification that employs replace and insert operations to generate perturbations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b53d4fc8498b22f926c2a8c3d0057326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adversarial Attacks">
      <data key="d0">Adversarial Attacks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adversarial Attacks are techniques that generate subtle input perturbations to cause machine learning models to misclassify inputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Adversarial attacks are a category of methods designed to generate perturbed inputs that induce errors or evaluate robustness in neural networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b6bd8ee29f5ff509c2fa42f7d1d1739&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b53d4fc8498b22f926c2a8c3d0057326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TextFooler">
      <data key="d0">TextFooler</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TextFooler is a baseline adversarial attack technique that performs synonym replacement within a fixed word embedding space.&lt;SEP&gt;TextFooler is a baseline black-box adversarial attack method that relies on rule-based synonym replacement within a fixed word embedding space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b53d4fc8498b22f926c2a8c3d0057326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Masked Language Model">
      <data key="d0">Masked Language Model</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Masked Language Model is a class of language representation models that predicts masked tokens based on surrounding context, leveraged to preserve sentence semantics and grammar.&lt;SEP&gt;Masked Language Model (MLM) is a self-supervised pre-training objective where a percentage of input tokens are masked and predicted to learn contextual representations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9b6bd8ee29f5ff509c2fa42f7d1d1739&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b53d4fc8498b22f926c2a8c3d0057326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hunalign">
      <data key="d0">Hunalign</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hunalign is a text alignment algorithm referenced in the context of alignment quality confidence scoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b53d4fc8498b22f926c2a8c3d0057326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Machine Learning">
      <data key="d0">Machine Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A branch of Artificial Intelligence that enables machines to learn and accomplish tasks without being explicitly programmed.&lt;SEP&gt;A branch of artificial intelligence and algorithmic modeling combined with lexicons to build predictive sentiment classifiers.&lt;SEP&gt;A branch of computational data analysis and artificial intelligence underlying food recognition, volume estimation, and cross-modal dietary assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;A computational approach planned to be integrated with dictionary-based matching to improve text classification and medical code assignment.&lt;SEP&gt;An automated computational approach that trains classification models on labeled datasets to classify unseen texts.&lt;SEP&gt;Machine Learning (ML) encompasses computational algorithms and models whose vulnerability to adversarial perturbations is explored in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Machine learning is the computational domain encompassing sentiment classification models, training datasets, and performance evaluation metrics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e6482c4aa158143b58b33b61435191e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b53d4fc8498b22f926c2a8c3d0057326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ced0d535e26b1f8838eba4645dd259b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f37521f7c9c4230a9fe0c9f8b7f1604&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c53d0ac7fa47d30ce6f399d3ef8b95e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c672772b311a70f628b5b6b878eb3f37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8756bb2d18f7993da0c11080bc5f15a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Black-Box Attack">
      <data key="d0">Black-Box Attack</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Black-Box Attack is an adversarial attack methodology where the attacker only queries the target model for output scores or probabilities without access to internal weights or gradients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b53d4fc8498b22f926c2a8c3d0057326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gradient-Based Approaches">
      <data key="d0">Gradient-Based Approaches</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gradient-Based Approaches are adversarial generation methods that rely on backpropagation and gradient calculation, facing challenges in discrete text spaces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b53d4fc8498b22f926c2a8c3d0057326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Generator Model Approaches">
      <data key="d0">Generator Model Approaches</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Generator Model Approaches are adversarial generation techniques utilizing generative models, which struggle with backpropagating perturbations from continuous embedding spaces to discrete token spaces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b53d4fc8498b22f926c2a8c3d0057326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Synonym Replacement">
      <data key="d0">Synonym Replacement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Synonym Replacement is a text perturbation strategy that swaps words with synonyms from a fixed embedding or dictionary, often causing unnatural phrasing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b53d4fc8498b22f926c2a8c3d0057326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Token Importance Estimation">
      <data key="d0">Token Importance Estimation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Token Importance Estimation is an analytical procedure in adversarial attacks that scores the impact of individual tokens by deleting them and measuring the prediction drop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b53d4fc8498b22f926c2a8c3d0057326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Replace Operation">
      <data key="d0">Replace Operation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Replace Operation is a perturbation action in BAE that substitutes an important token with a masked token filled by a language model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b53d4fc8498b22f926c2a8c3d0057326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insert Operation">
      <data key="d0">Insert Operation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Insert Operation is a perturbation action in BAE that adds an adjacent token using masked language model predictions to alter sentence context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b53d4fc8498b22f926c2a8c3d0057326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Amazon">
      <data key="d0">Amazon</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A major technology company that developed the Amazon Echo smart agent.&lt;SEP&gt;Amazon is an American technology and e-commerce company whose customer review data is utilized as a benchmark dataset for sentiment classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Amazon is an e-commerce company whose customer reviews are utilized as a source of external world facts to inform conversational agents.&lt;SEP&gt;An e-commerce and technology company whose customer reviews are referenced as an external knowledge source.&lt;SEP&gt;An e-commerce platform whose customer review dataset is utilized for evaluating adversarial attacks and sentiment classification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b32cb050de56f58b5792720c6bc3e42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f7cfb64157b0b1becf1f72e036758aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18770431e1f17cd9efc7aedcdb98d303</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yelp">
      <data key="d0">Yelp</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A crowd-sourced review platform whose dataset is utilized to test the effectiveness of adversarial text attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Yelp is a local business review and social networking platform whose review data forms a sentiment classification dataset evaluated in the study.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMDB">
      <data key="d0">IMDB</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An online database and platform related to films and entertainment whose review dataset is used as a benchmark for text classification experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;An online movie database whose review dataset is used to benchmark adversarial attacks and sentiment analysis models.&lt;SEP&gt;IMDB (Internet Movie Database) is an online database of movie information and user reviews whose sentiment classification dataset serves as a benchmark in text classification.&lt;SEP&gt;IMDB is an online database of film and entertainment media whose movie review dataset is used for sentence-level sentiment classification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-784f828261b96cc3c6df35aff5b1cae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f5016d3aa81277cabc5c947f1ca64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TREC">
      <data key="d0">TREC</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Text REtrieval Conference (TREC) is an ongoing series of workshop competitions and events providing benchmark datasets for question type classification.&lt;SEP&gt;The Text REtrieval Conference, an ongoing workshop series whose question classification dataset is used to evaluate model robustness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Universal Sentence Encoder">
      <data key="d0">Universal Sentence Encoder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Universal Sentence Encoder (USE) is an embedding-based model utilized to compute semantic similarity scores to filter adversarial perturbations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Word-LSTM">
      <data key="d0">Word-LSTM</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Word-LSTM is a recurrent neural network architecture evaluated as a target model for text classification under adversarial attacks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Word-CNN">
      <data key="d0">Word-CNN</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Word-CNN is a convolutional neural network architecture evaluated as a target model for text classification under adversarial attacks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Subjectivity Detection">
      <data key="d0">Subjectivity Detection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Subjectivity detection is a text classification task category focused on determining whether a sentence is subjective or objective.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Question Type Classification">
      <data key="d0">Question Type Classification</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Question type classification is a task category aimed at categorizing user questions into specific intent or topic classes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Opinion Polarity Detection">
      <data key="d0">Opinion Polarity Detection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Opinion polarity detection is a natural language processing task category aimed at identifying the polarity of opinions in text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BAE-R">
      <data key="d0">BAE-R</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BAE-R is an attack mode of BAE that replaces a given token with a predicted token using the BERT masked language model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BAE-I">
      <data key="d0">BAE-I</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BAE-I is an attack mode of BAE that inserts a predicted token to the left or right of a given token.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BAE-R/I">
      <data key="d0">BAE-R/I</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BAE-R/I is an attack mode of BAE that either replaces a token or inserts a token adjacent to it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BAE-R+I">
      <data key="d0">BAE-R+I</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BAE-R+I is the strongest attack mode of BAE that first replaces a token and then inserts a token to its left or right.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MR">
      <data key="d0">MR</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MR (Movie Reviews) is a sentiment polarity benchmark dataset containing movie reviews used for evaluating text classification models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MPQA">
      <data key="d0">MPQA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MPQA is a text classification benchmark dataset used for opinion polarity detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Subj">
      <data key="d0">Subj</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Subj is a benchmark dataset used for subjectivity detection to classify whether a sentence is subjective or objective.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-32e33b95f1377956f09b6af9eda02260</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NLP Community">
      <data key="d0">NLP Community</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The collective group of researchers and practitioners working in the field of Natural Language Processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentiment Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Sentiment Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A computational classification task aimed at identifying attitudes, opinions, and polarity expressed in natural language texts like tweets.&lt;SEP&gt;A computational field and natural language processing discipline concerned with identifying and categorizing opinions and attitudes expressed in text.&lt;SEP&gt;A major field of natural language processing focused on computationally identifying and categorizing opinions and sentiments expressed in text.&lt;SEP&gt;A subfield of shallow semantic text analysis focused on extracting opinions, emotions, and attitudes towards specific entities or topics.&lt;SEP&gt;A text classification sub-task focused on determining the affective state, opinion, or subjective emotion expressed in text.&lt;SEP&gt;An NLP subfield analyzing subjective affective states, cited as an area benefiting from transfer learning.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentiment Analysis is a broader field in natural language processing aimed at computationally identifying and categorizing opinions and emotions expressed in text.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentiment Analysis is an NLP task focused on identifying and classifying opinions and sentimental polarity in texts.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentiment analysis is a computational technique measuring user sentiment that serves as an indirect signal for real-world time series and prediction tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentiment analysis is a natural language processing task focused on determining the subjective tone, emotion, or polarity in text.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentiment analysis is a subfield of natural language processing concerned with identifying and classifying the emotional tone or polarity expressed in text.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentiment analysis is the field and process of computationally identifying and categorizing opinions and emotions expressed in text.&lt;SEP&gt;The computational study and identification of opinions, emotional polarity, and sentiments in text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b98e14081bd42f130f547ac6df8114f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b5684eaac7a3f52271bc1ff14d5ff1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b31134945186e62e7effccb3ba89b180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97953bb0d586e46165fea699da6cf781&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e060c5bbcfa40d71aa9fb0a2e63bff30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-289c3be854417f1af5a604180e6310cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c672772b311a70f628b5b6b878eb3f37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-707d9bb0e2896feb599f211f8cc655a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8756bb2d18f7993da0c11080bc5f15a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d95ef3b7585c8c501112f173f330c95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47f43d3218257ab88ccde621eadece2c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aspect-Level Sentiment Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Aspect-Level Sentiment Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A fine-grained subfield of sentiment analysis focused on identifying sentiment polarities toward specific aspects or components of entities.&lt;SEP&gt;A subfield of sentiment analysis focusing on identifying sentiments related to specific aspects or entities within a text.&lt;SEP&gt;A subtask of natural language processing and sentiment analysis that focuses on identifying opinion holders, targets, and sentiment polarities at the aspect level.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cross-Lingual Sentiment Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Cross-Lingual Sentiment Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A subfield of natural language processing focusing on transferring sentiment analysis capabilities across multiple languages.&lt;SEP&gt;A subfield of sentiment analysis dealing with transferring sentiment models and analysis across different languages.&lt;SEP&gt;A subfield of sentiment analysis focused on analyzing sentiment across different languages, often through unsupervised or semi-supervised approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adversarial Attack">
      <data key="d0">Adversarial Attack</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A technique involving small, intentional perturbations to text inputs designed to cause machine learning models to make incorrect predictions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Basque">
      <data key="d0">Basque</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A co-official language spoken in the Iberian Peninsula, traditionally under-resourced in aspect-level sentiment corpora.&lt;SEP&gt;A language with a rich morphological system, featured in the MultiBooked corpus for aspect-level sentiment analysis and multilingual research.&lt;SEP&gt;An under-resourced language for which new annotated datasets are introduced for supervised aspect-level sentiment classification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Catalan">
      <data key="d0">Catalan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Romance language featured in the newly constructed morphological translation dictionaries.&lt;SEP&gt;A co-official language spoken in the Iberian Peninsula, traditionally under-resourced in aspect-level sentiment corpora.&lt;SEP&gt;A language with a rich morphological system, included in the MultiBooked corpus for aspect-level sentiment analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;An under-resourced language for which new annotated datasets are provided for aspect-level sentiment analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Catalan is a Romance language examined in the bilingual lexicon induction experiments, specifically in the Catalan–Portuguese language pair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00381e9f572e2f4c1655790b19b827fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a857415cf32d5d04b955b0da8d697209</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Media Sentiment Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Social Media Sentiment Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A subfield of sentiment analysis focusing on extracting sentiments and opinions from social media platforms and tweets.&lt;SEP&gt;A subfield of sentiment analysis that focuses on extracting and interpreting opinions and emotional tone from social media content.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multi-Lingual Sentiment Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Multi-Lingual Sentiment Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A research area within sentiment analysis addressing sentiment classification across multiple languages, often via unsupervised or semi-supervised methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Subj Dataset">
      <data key="d0">Subj Dataset</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A standard text classification dataset focused on subjectivity detection, evaluated for robustness against adversarial perturbation operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MultiBooked Corpus">
      <data key="d0">MultiBooked Corpus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A corpus composed of Basque and Catalan hotel reviews annotated for supervised aspect-level sentiment classification.&lt;SEP&gt;A corpus of hotel reviews annotated for aspect-level sentiment analysis, available in Basque and Catalan to facilitate supervised and multilingual sentiment analysis research.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Question Classification">
      <data key="d0">Question Classification</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A natural language processing task exemplified by the TREC benchmark dataset to test sentence classification models.&lt;SEP&gt;Question classification (represented by the QC dataset) is an NLP task category centered on identifying the type or intent of questions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-16a3ad946a112f7d6ba66cf07c1458d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f5016d3aa81277cabc5c947f1ca64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iberian Peninsula">
      <data key="d0">Iberian Peninsula</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A geographical region containing official languages such as Spanish and Portuguese, as well as co-official languages like Basque, Catalan, and Galician.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Booking.com">
      <data key="d0">Booking.com</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An online travel reservation platform from which Catalan hotel reviews were collected for sentiment corpus creation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kyoto Project">
      <data key="d0">Kyoto Project</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A research initiative that originated the KAF/NAF stand-off XML format used to store multilayered linguistic annotations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OpeNER Project">
      <data key="d0">OpeNER Project</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A European language technology initiative that defined annotation schemes and resources for sentiment analysis used as a reference framework.&lt;SEP&gt;The OpeNER project is a research initiative whose opinion annotation guidelines and scheme were adopted to annotate text spans and sentiment labels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74fa75c4a6b2b3f3b953b4f392d8136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spanish">
      <data key="d0">Spanish</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Romance language evaluated in BLI experiments, both with other Romance languages and with the unrelated language Polish.&lt;SEP&gt;A Romance language evaluated in language pairs with French and Polish, characterized by lack of nominal case declension and large verbal paradigms.&lt;SEP&gt;A Romance language evaluated in the cross-lingual morphological datasets and MUSE comparison.&lt;SEP&gt;A language used in child model experiments to compare the effects of different parent languages, specifically French and German.&lt;SEP&gt;A widely spoken majority language prevalent across diverse demographics in both datasets.&lt;SEP&gt;An official language of the Iberian Peninsula with established sentiment analysis resources at both tweet and aspect levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Spanish is a Romance language analyzed in the context of morphological generalization and translation dictionaries.&lt;SEP&gt;Spanish is a Romance language analyzed in the study as part of the French–Spanish language pair.&lt;SEP&gt;Spanish is a natural language tested in a low-resource Spanish–English translation experiment to evaluate the effect of parent model language choice.&lt;SEP&gt;Spanish is a widely used language identified as very frequent on Twitter and noted among false-positive languages detected in digital corpora.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6152391765893a69aa07b46696567745&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7818b9847aa576cb0d1559c6c5e963ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ca3ab8f8c0dca0a40d79a8ee4226255&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-874a0fecf572a7885d7353d9aa510123&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7d53ab63937232bbcc4be9c7080dd31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9896f0582e2237c4a0dd8169f6817110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c17cbdd46b3ef569862a8fa188b4fab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00381e9f572e2f4c1655790b19b827fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a857415cf32d5d04b955b0da8d697209</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Portuguese">
      <data key="d0">Portuguese</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Romance language included in the bilingual morphological induction dataset and MUSE evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;An official language of the Iberian Peninsula with available tweet-level sentiment resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Portuguese is a Romance language analyzed in the bilingual lexicon induction task as part of the Catalan–Portuguese pair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00381e9f572e2f4c1655790b19b827fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a857415cf32d5d04b955b0da8d697209</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Galician">
      <data key="d0">Galician</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A co-official language of the Iberian Peninsula noted for having very few sentiment analysis resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aragonese">
      <data key="d0">Aragonese</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A minority language spoken in the Iberian Peninsula lacking sentiment analysis corpora.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gascon">
      <data key="d0">Gascon</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A smaller language spoken in the Iberian Peninsula with minimal linguistic resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Document-Level Sentiment Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Document-Level Sentiment Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A granularity of sentiment analysis focused on evaluating overall sentiment across complete documents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentence-Level Sentiment Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Sentence-Level Sentiment Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A level of sentiment analysis aimed at determining sentiment polarity within individual sentences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KAF/NAF Format">
      <data key="d0">KAF/NAF Format</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A stand-off XML linguistic representation format originated by the Kyoto project that allows multi-layer text annotation without altering raw text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ixa-pipes">
      <data key="d0">Ixa-pipes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A set of modular natural language processing tools used to perform tokenization, POS-tagging, and lemmatization on crawled text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a29973c98d2c28f9d2efe87b89d1943f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Opinion Holder">
      <data key="d0">Opinion Holder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A semantic annotation category referring to the span of text identifying the person or entity who expresses or holds an opinion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74fa75c4a6b2b3f3b953b4f392d8136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Opinion Target">
      <data key="d0">Opinion Target</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An annotation category representing the entity or specific aspect of a hotel that is being evaluated in a review.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74fa75c4a6b2b3f3b953b4f392d8136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Opinion Expression">
      <data key="d0">Opinion Expression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An annotation category referring to the textual span that conveys an attitude or subjective evaluation towards an aspect.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74fa75c4a6b2b3f3b953b4f392d8136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polarity">
      <data key="d0">Polarity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sentiment classification category assigned to opinion expressions, categorized into strong negative, negative, positive, or strong positive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74fa75c4a6b2b3f3b953b4f392d8136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hotel Reviews">
      <data key="d0">Hotel Reviews</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The text domain and corpus genre consisting of customer evaluations of hotels, serving as the source data for annotation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74fa75c4a6b2b3f3b953b4f392d8136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Catalan Corpus">
      <data key="d0">Catalan Corpus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A dataset comprising 567 annotated hotel reviews in the Catalan language created using the OpeNER annotation methodology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74fa75c4a6b2b3f3b953b4f392d8136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Basque Corpus">
      <data key="d0">Basque Corpus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A dataset comprising 343 annotated hotel reviews in the Basque language created using the OpeNER annotation methodology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74fa75c4a6b2b3f3b953b4f392d8136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KafAnnotator Tool">
      <data key="d0">KafAnnotator Tool</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A software tool used to annotate reviews by allowing users to select token spans and assign them specific labels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74fa75c4a6b2b3f3b953b4f392d8136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agr Metric">
      <data key="d0">Agr Metric</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An inter-annotator agreement evaluation metric applied to sequence annotations to compute precision, recall, and overall consensus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74fa75c4a6b2b3f3b953b4f392d8136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cohen's Kappa">
      <data key="d0">Cohen's Kappa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A traditional statistical metric for inter-annotator agreement noted as inapplicable to arbitrary sequence span annotations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74fa75c4a6b2b3f3b953b4f392d8136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fleiss' Kappa">
      <data key="d0">Fleiss' Kappa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A multi-rater inter-annotator agreement metric noted as unsuitable for evaluating free sequence span selections.&lt;SEP&gt;A statistical measure used to calculate inter-annotator agreement among multiple raters evaluating categorical data.&lt;SEP&gt;Fleiss' Kappa is a statistical measure used to calculate inter-annotator agreement among multiple annotators on categorical items.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef7b95a2f35faad97942f87543b7ab1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f74fa75c4a6b2b3f3b953b4f392d8136&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-362346ad5b3a18dced3a68b0e282ea62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Annotation Process">
      <data key="d0">Annotation Process</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A multi-phase procedure comprising iterative annotation rounds, comparison sessions, discussions, and conflict resolution by a third annotator.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f74fa75c4a6b2b3f3b953b4f392d8136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multilingual Natural Language Processing">
      <data key="d0">Multilingual Natural Language Processing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A subfield of natural language processing focused on developing models capable of understanding and processing multiple languages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neural Language Models">
      <data key="d0">Neural Language Models</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A class of artificial intelligence models that use neural networks to model language, compute joint probabilities of word sequences, and predict upcoming words.&lt;SEP&gt;Probabilistic computational models of human language implemented using neural networks, serving as core components in diverse NLP applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37ae86766195404daaa0c376964357ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Image Captioning">
      <data key="d0">Image Captioning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A multimodal AI task focused on generating natural language descriptions of images using encoder-decoder neural network architectures.&lt;SEP&gt;A multimodal artificial intelligence task generating textual descriptions for visual images, leveraging neural language models.&lt;SEP&gt;Image captioning is a multimodal task in which artificial intelligence models generate textual descriptions of visual scenes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f76f1c0c2ed37a786263a6a6d8b0bb6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6e4431e2ead79e10a12d16d49f4d480</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LREC">
      <data key="d0">LREC</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Language Resources and Evaluation Conference, a major academic conference focused on language resources and NLP technologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conditional Random Field">
      <data key="d0">Conditional Random Field</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A class of statistical modeling methods applied in pattern recognition and machine learning for structured prediction in entity and attribute extraction.&lt;SEP&gt;A statistical modeling method used for supervised sequence labeling to extract opinion holders, targets, and expressions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f76f1c0c2ed37a786263a6a6d8b0bb6d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Linear SVM Classifier">
      <data key="d0">Linear SVM Classifier</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A machine learning model trained on Bag-of-Words features to classify the sentiment polarity of opinion expressions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence Labeling">
      <data key="d0">Sequence Labeling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A standard natural language processing task framework used to identify spans of opinion holders, targets, and expressions via token-level tags.&lt;SEP&gt;Sequence Labeling is a type of pattern recognition and natural language processing task where a categorical label is assigned to each token in an input sequence, commonly used for aspect extraction.&lt;SEP&gt;Sequence labeling is a machine learning task category where tokens in an input sequence are classified into assigned categorical labels, such as the B-I-O tagging scheme.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b6bd8ee29f5ff509c2fa42f7d1d1739&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47f43d3218257ab88ccde621eadece2c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bag-of-Words">
      <data key="d0">Bag-of-Words</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A feature representation technique in NLP used to represent text based on word occurrence for training classifiers.&lt;SEP&gt;Bag-of-Words is a text representation technique commonly used to engineer features for sentiment classification models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3194fa4581f2aaec823530e030775b2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Language Vectors">
      <data key="d0">Language Vectors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Continuous real-valued vector representations of languages used in neural models to capture genetic linguistic relationships and address data sparseness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Character-Based Neural Language Model">
      <data key="d0">Character-Based Neural Language Model</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A neural network language modeling architecture operating on character sequences, used to learn continuous language vectors efficiently.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bible Translations">
      <data key="d0">Bible Translations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A massively multilingual parallel dataset comprising 1,303 translations across 990 languages used to explore continuous multilingual representations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58403a931bb26f8c20e17e82757f13f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Johnson">
      <data key="d0">Johnson</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who, concurrently in 2016, conducted a study utilizing neural machine translation with language identifier tokens.&lt;SEP&gt;A researcher whose 2016 zero-shot learning findings are referenced in relation to vector interpolation between languages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ammar">
      <data key="d0">Ammar</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who, in 2016, utilized one-hot language identifiers as input to a multilingual word-based dependency parser.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wichmann">
      <data key="d0">Wichmann</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher who studied automatic language classification in 2010.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Universal Dependencies">
      <data key="d0">Universal Dependencies</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An initiative and open-source project providing a diverse collection of multilingual treebanks used for syntactic parsing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bible Translations Corpus">
      <data key="d0">Bible Translations Corpus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A massively multilingual collection of 1,303 Bible translations spanning 990 languages used as training and evaluation data for the language models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multilingual Language Models">
      <data key="d0">Multilingual Language Models</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A class of natural language processing models trained across multiple languages using shared network parameters and language embeddings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Latin Script">
      <data key="d0">Latin Script</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A widely used alphabetic script for which custom orthographic syllabification rules were applied in the experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;A writing system script filter applied to translations included in the training corpus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5cf9480461a20dfddfe7c1ed32cfa07c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cyrillic Script">
      <data key="d0">Cyrillic Script</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A writing system script filter applied to translations included in the training corpus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greek Script">
      <data key="d0">Greek Script</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A writing system script filter applied to translations included in the training corpus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stacked Character-Based LSTM">
      <data key="d0">Stacked Character-Based LSTM</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A neural network architecture with two LSTM layers used as the foundational language model to process multilingual character sequences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adam Optimizer">
      <data key="d0">Adam Optimizer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adam Optimizer is an adaptive learning rate optimization algorithm utilized during neural network model training.&lt;SEP&gt;Adam is an adaptive learning rate optimization algorithm used to train the Char2Vec model parameters over multiple epochs.&lt;SEP&gt;Adam optimizer is an optimization algorithm category utilized for training deep learning models with adaptive learning rates.&lt;SEP&gt;An adaptive learning rate optimization algorithm used for training neural network models including G-SAT.&lt;SEP&gt;An optimization algorithm category used to train the activity prediction models across training epochs.&lt;SEP&gt;An optimization algorithm utilized for updating neural network weights during the model's training process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-de8f6faf25c75fd8dcf0e4d67e379249&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-275b54efb44b71f0c8102cd8da9ac971&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0f9c89ffb57d88b6a05fbcd7d909d7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67aef3e225567bceaa8ddbf5e9866527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af6147035553ac26ef0837a5ab654f4f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Layer Normalization">
      <data key="d0">Layer Normalization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A regularization and normalization technique applied to neural network layers to stabilize training across billions of characters.&lt;SEP&gt;Layer Normalization (LN) is a normalization technique applied within multi-head self-attention layers to stabilize training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8273db0372f06b5474e49c6b558bba7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="K40 GPU">
      <data key="d0">K40 GPU</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A graphics processing unit hardware accelerator utilized to execute the model training process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hierarchical Agglomerative Clustering">
      <data key="d0">Hierarchical Agglomerative Clustering</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An algorithmic clustering methodology utilized to visualize and evaluate the organization of the multilingual vector space.&lt;SEP&gt;An unsupervised clustering method applied to explore and visualize the structure of the learned language vector space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Testament">
      <data key="d0">New Testament</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A major section of the Bible that makes up the majority of the translated texts in the experimental corpus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Old Testament">
      <data key="d0">Old Testament</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A portion of the Bible present in roughly 300 translations of the multilingual corpus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISO 639-3">
      <data key="d0">ISO 639-3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An international standard code system used to distinguish and treat historical and distinct language varieties separately.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multilingual Dependency Parser">
      <data key="d0">Multilingual Dependency Parser</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A word-based parsing model that leverages one-hot language identifiers and multilingual word embeddings to encode syntactic features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Language Embeddings">
      <data key="d0">Language Embeddings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dense continuous vector representations learned for each language to capture linguistic and typographic properties in vector space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-058efe22b46081832638e1629ec8cd5b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="King James">
      <data key="d0">King James</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">King James is an English monarch associated with the King James Bible, which is referenced as a source of text data for testing modern and middle English language models.&lt;SEP&gt;The historical monarch associated with the 1611 translation of the Christian Bible, used as intermediate linguistic text in the study.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Publication of the King James Bible">
      <data key="d0">Publication of the King James Bible</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The 1611 publication of the King James Version of the Bible, serving as an intermediate historical benchmark between Middle and Modern English.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germanic Languages">
      <data key="d0">Germanic Languages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A branch of the Indo-European language family whose conventional analytical and parsing methods are often not directly transferable to languages like Korean.&lt;SEP&gt;A language family whose subset is used to demonstrate hierarchical clustering and genetic language relationships in the vector space.&lt;SEP&gt;Germanic languages are a language family group whose language vectors and multi-language cross-entropy performance are analyzed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18770431e1f17cd9efc7aedcdb98d303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="West Germanic Languages">
      <data key="d0">West Germanic Languages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A subgroup within the Germanic language family that traditionally includes languages such as English and German.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="North Germanic Languages">
      <data key="d0">North Germanic Languages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A subgroup within the Germanic language family comprising Scandinavian languages like Danish, Norwegian, and Swedish.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Romance Languages">
      <data key="d0">Romance Languages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A branch of Indo-European languages including French and Spanish, noted for large verbal paradigms consisting of more than 60 forms.&lt;SEP&gt;A language family including French, Spanish, Italian, Portuguese, and Catalan, analyzed for inflectional mapping and cross-lingual induction.&lt;SEP&gt;A language family noted in the text for having exerted significant historical influence on the English language.&lt;SEP&gt;A major European language family containing Spanish, analyzed through morphologically complete dictionaries in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Romance Languages is a linguistic group analyzed in the bilingual lexicon induction experiments to assess model translation accuracy across grammatical forms.&lt;SEP&gt;The Romance language family consists of genetically-related languages such as Spanish and Italian, used in creating morphologically complete dictionaries for bilingual lexicon induction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0ca3ab8f8c0dca0a40d79a8ee4226255&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-874a0fecf572a7885d7353d9aa510123&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9896f0582e2237c4a0dd8169f6817110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00381e9f572e2f4c1655790b19b827fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a857415cf32d5d04b955b0da8d697209&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Celtic Languages">
      <data key="d0">Celtic Languages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A language family cited as possessing more distant genetic relationships to the Germanic languages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modern English">
      <data key="d0">Modern English</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Modern English is a linguistic stage of the English language tested on biblical text using interpolated language models.&lt;SEP&gt;The linguistic stage of the English language from 1500 onward, used as an endpoint in vector interpolation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Middle English">
      <data key="d0">Middle English</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Middle English is a historical linguistic stage of the English language evaluated on text from the King James Bible.&lt;SEP&gt;The historical stage of the English language from 1050 to 1500, used as an endpoint in language vector interpolation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Danish">
      <data key="d0">Danish</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A North Germanic language evaluated alongside varieties of Norwegian in hierarchical clustering experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Danish is a Scandinavian language incorporated alongside Norwegian in multilingual language modeling benchmarks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Norwegian">
      <data key="d0">Norwegian</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A North Germanic language with two varieties analyzed for stability within the language vector hierarchy.&lt;SEP&gt;Norwegian is a Scandinavian language evaluated in the step-by-step cross-entropy language model tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Swedish">
      <data key="d0">Swedish</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A North Germanic language evaluated in language model capacity experiments when adding closely related languages.&lt;SEP&gt;Swedish is a North Germanic language analyzed in cross-entropy evaluations and language vector scaling experiments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="King James Version of the Bible">
      <data key="d0">King James Version of the Bible</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A prominent 1611 English biblical text translation serving as a linguistic corpus representing an intermediate state between Middle English and Modern English.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Languages">
      <data key="d0">Natural Languages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The domain of human spoken and written communication systems represented by 990 dense language vectors in the study.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Language Family Trees">
      <data key="d0">Language Family Trees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phylogenetic classifications of linguistic lineages reconstructed through clustering dense vector representations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training of the 990-Language Model">
      <data key="d0">Training of the 990-Language Model</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The large-scale computational training process on raw text across 990 natural languages to produce dense language vectors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Language Vector Interpolation Experiment">
      <data key="d0">Language Vector Interpolation Experiment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The experimental procedure testing cross-entropy and text generation across linear paths between distinct language vectors in vector space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Language Identification Experiment">
      <data key="d0">Language Identification Experiment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An experimental optimization trial estimating a language vector by freezing model weights and backpropagating with a small set of 32 sentences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ebac0f3b43edbff22438f6ec756f16fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scandinavia">
      <data key="d0">Scandinavia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Scandinavia is a geographical and cultural region in Northern Europe associated with the Scandinavian language family evaluated in language modeling experiments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SemEval-2010">
      <data key="d0">SemEval-2010</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">SemEval-2010 is an academic evaluation workshop and benchmark dataset comprising computer science research papers used for keyphrase extraction and generation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DUC Evaluation Campaign">
      <data key="d0">DUC Evaluation Campaign</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An evaluation campaign focused on text summarization for which the DUC-2001 dataset was originally created.&lt;SEP&gt;The DUC evaluation campaign is a series of benchmark evaluations focused on text summarization, which produced the DUC-2001 news dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5179247480b7af630790fe26e5e9372c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Keyphrase Generation">
      <data key="d0">Keyphrase Generation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Keyphrase Generation is a natural language processing task and category focused on producing both present and missing keyphrases for a given document.&lt;SEP&gt;Keyphrase Generation is an NLP task focused on predicting or generating key phrases representing documents.&lt;SEP&gt;Keyphrase generation is a natural language processing task that predicts a set of lexical units conveying the core content of a source document.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Keyphrase Extraction">
      <data key="d0">Keyphrase Extraction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Keyphrase Extraction is an information processing category and task restricted to identifying and extracting keyphrases directly appearing in document texts.&lt;SEP&gt;Keyphrase extraction is an information retrieval and NLP task that identifies keyphrases appearing directly in the source document.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Text Summarization">
      <data key="d0">Text Summarization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Text summarization is a broad natural language processing task of creating condensed representations of texts, closely related to keyphrase generation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991</data>
    </node>
    <node id="News Domain">
      <data key="d0">News Domain</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The news domain is a textual category consisting of journalistic articles used for faceted navigation, topic tracking, and keyphrase generation evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f7a0c631645254ecce4e14a53d44a991</data>
    </node>
