    </node>
    <node id="Ygorg">
      <data key="d0">Ygorg</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Ygorg is the developer and researcher associated with the repository hosting the KPTimes dataset and implementation on GitHub.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KPTimes">
      <data key="d0">KPTimes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">KPTimes is a large-scale benchmark dataset category composed of newswire articles used to train and test keyphrase generation models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KP20k">
      <data key="d0">KP20k</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">KP20k is a benchmark dataset category consisting of scientific abstracts used for training and evaluating keyphrase extraction and generation models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="News Summarization">
      <data key="d0">News Summarization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">News Summarization is a natural language processing task category aimed at generating concise summaries from news articles, often utilizing lead baselines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Newswire">
      <data key="d0">Newswire</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Newswire is a textual domain and genre category characterized by journalistic reporting, editorial metadata, and lead-focused sentence structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scientific Documents">
      <data key="d0">Scientific Documents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Scientific Documents is a textual domain category encompassing scholarly papers and abstracts characterized by technical, domain-specific terminology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="JPTimes">
      <data key="d0">JPTimes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">JPTimes is a news domain benchmark dataset featuring editor-assigned keyphrases used to evaluate keyphrase generation models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KPCrowd">
      <data key="d0">KPCrowd</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">KPCrowd is a benchmark dataset annotated by readers, characterized by a low ratio of absent keyphrases and used for evaluating keyphrase extraction models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CopyRNN">
      <data key="d0">CopyRNN</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CopyRNN is a state-of-the-art supervised neural generative encoder-decoder model that incorporates a copying mechanism to extract and generate keyphrases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CopyNews">
      <data key="d0">CopyNews</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CopyNews is an instance of the CopyRNN generative model trained specifically on newswire articles from the KPTimes dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CopySci">
      <data key="d0">CopySci</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CopySci is an instance of the CopyRNN generative model trained on scientific abstracts from the KP20k dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MultipartiteRank">
      <data key="d0">MultipartiteRank</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MultipartiteRank is an unsupervised, graph-based keyphrase extraction model that enforces topical diversity when ranking candidate phrases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FirstPhrases">
      <data key="d0">FirstPhrases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FirstPhrases is a positional heuristic baseline model for keyphrase extraction that selects the first N keyphrase candidates appearing in a document.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pke">
      <data key="d0">Pke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pke is an open-source Python keyphrase extraction toolkit that provides an implementation of the MultipartiteRank model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Language Generation">
      <data key="d0">Natural Language Generation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A dialogue system component responsible for generating natural language utterances returned as system responses to the user.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of NLP focused on converting a communication goal into coherent natural language form for dialogue systems.&lt;SEP&gt;A subfield of artificial intelligence and computational linguistics focused on generating natural language texts from underlying non-linguistic data, concepts, or ontologies.&lt;SEP&gt;A subfield of artificial intelligence and computational linguistics focused on producing coherent natural language texts from underlying data, ontologies, or semantic representations.&lt;SEP&gt;A subfield of artificial intelligence focused on producing natural language texts from structured data or ontologies.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural Language Generation (NLG) is a subfield of artificial intelligence and computational linguistics focused on automatically generating coherent natural language text from non-linguistic data.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural Language Generation (NLG) is a subfield of artificial intelligence and computational linguistics focused on producing natural language texts from non-linguistic or structured data.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural Language Generation is an overarching category and discipline within natural language processing encompassing summarization, machine translation, and keyphrase generation.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural language generation is a software process that produces natural language output from structured data or intermediate semantic representations.&lt;SEP&gt;The computational task and process of automatically generating coherent and concise natural language texts from structured facts or data.&lt;SEP&gt;The subfield of artificial intelligence and computational linguistics concerned with producing coherent natural language text from semantic representations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd9314a3266fc8d0bc21af4b765fa25b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99df0a2940e224839126173e8c2bc846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b6907deaed642269724a32b0c9ccd9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6575c1afc788be74bd028dfc616620&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a12c0c800f66f36ecdd6e9a143afe7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa79759ff02333ae3d3a1d802134a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea9c29cc7a5d0f200ae552bfc4494ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfd4e1f2af27018cbd2ff675e9601171&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fcb14d19cf40f8290f78e99be61a949&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-785a16b34b0c74a5ee4f9f416976a683&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa0a1d40aed971d10ad0f0f2c4938cf4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Navigli">
      <data key="d0">Navigli</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Navigli is a researcher and author noted for publishing a comprehensive survey on word sense disambiguation algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SensEval-3">
      <data key="d0">SensEval-3</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An evaluation benchmark and task in natural language processing used to evaluate word sense disambiguation models, featuring lexical sample tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;SensEval-3 is a computational linguistics evaluation workshop and benchmark event focused on word sense disambiguation tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;SensEval-3 is a natural language processing evaluation workshop and benchmark challenge that included a lexical sample task for evaluating Word Sense Disambiguation systems.&lt;SEP&gt;SensEval-3 is an evaluation exercise and benchmark event in natural language processing used to evaluate word sense disambiguation algorithms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2a718261409ed817eb2c8548c15249f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supervised WSD Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Supervised WSD Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Supervised WSD algorithms are a class of word sense disambiguation techniques that induce classification models from labeled training examples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Knowledge-Based WSD Approaches">
      <data key="d0">Knowledge-Based WSD Approaches</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Knowledge-based WSD approaches are a category of word sense disambiguation methods reliant on manually created lexical resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unsupervised WSD Algorithms">
      <data key="d0">Unsupervised WSD Algorithms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Unsupervised WSD algorithms are a class of disambiguation techniques that operate without labeled sense data, such as using topic modeling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomedicine">
      <data key="d0">Biomedicine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomedicine is a scientific and technical domain mentioned as a key application area for word sense disambiguation models and clinical report mining.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomedicine is a specialized scientific domain identified as a planned future evaluation area for testing domain adaptation of the single BLSTM WSD model.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomedicine is a technical domain referenced as a target application area for word sense disambiguation models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WordNet">
      <data key="d0">WordNet</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">WordNet is a lexical database for the English language used in natural language processing to expand and match property mentions.&lt;SEP&gt;WordNet is a lexical database project whose vocabulary was cross-referenced to generate out-of-vocabulary inflections for evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;WordNet is a lexical database used to retrieve synsets and frequencies of word senses to classify syntactic properties.&lt;SEP&gt;WordNet is a manually created lexical resource and semantic database widely used in knowledge-based word sense disambiguation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb73055a3a263fcef16382513853f022&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a857415cf32d5d04b955b0da8d697209&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5454e5b20bde9e381fa3cda34f91dec7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unified Medical Language System">
      <data key="d0">Unified Medical Language System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Unified Medical Language System (UMLS) is a biomedical knowledge resource and lexical ontology used in knowledge-based word sense disambiguation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Neural Networks">
      <data key="d0">Deep Neural Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of machine learning models, including recurrent and convolutional neural networks, representing the state-of-the-art approach for dialogue state tracking.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Neural Networks are layered machine learning architectures increasingly adopted for natural language processing and word sense disambiguation.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep Neural Networks is a broad class of machine learning models with multiple layers that learn hierarchical feature representations directly from input data.&lt;SEP&gt;Multi-layered artificial neural network architectures capable of feature extraction but prone to overfitting when trained on small labeled datasets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-26c91c9a531d8aea5394e79e584448ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-785a16b34b0c74a5ee4f9f416976a683&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d95ef3b7585c8c501112f173f330c95</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory">
      <data key="d0">Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BLSTM) is a neural network architecture designed to process sequence context in both directions, proposed here for collective word sense disambiguation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sense Embeddings">
      <data key="d0">Sense Embeddings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sense embeddings are dense neural vectors representing specific semantic senses of words, learned during model training.&lt;SEP&gt;Vector representations that encode individual semantic senses of words, providing distinct embeddings for different meanings of the same word.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626602288681ee562ad75771b687c6be</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topic Modeling">
      <data key="d0">Topic Modeling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A text analysis and statistical modeling approach used to discover latent semantic topics across text corpora.&lt;SEP&gt;Topic modeling represents unsupervised computational methods used to discover abstract semantic topics and disambiguate word senses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23f22646227380376cc403c82c1f4615&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-707d9bb0e2896feb599f211f8cc655a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hochreiter">
      <data key="d0">Hochreiter</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A computer scientist who, along with Schmidhuber in 1997, introduced the Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) gated recurrent neural network architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626602288681ee562ad75771b687c6be</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Schmidhuber">
      <data key="d0">Schmidhuber</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A computer scientist who, along with Hochreiter in 1997, introduced the Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) gated recurrent neural network architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626602288681ee562ad75771b687c6be</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chen Et Al.">
      <data key="d0">Chen Et Al.</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Researchers who adapted neural word embeddings to calculate distinct sense embeddings of the same word and evaluated their approach on SemEval-2007 data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626602288681ee562ad75771b687c6be</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SemEval-2007">
      <data key="d0">SemEval-2007</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An international workshop and shared task evaluation event for semantic evaluation, used to benchmark sense embedding and word sense disambiguation performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626602288681ee562ad75771b687c6be</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bidirectional LSTM">
      <data key="d0">Bidirectional LSTM</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A neural network architecture composed of two reversed unidirectional LSTMs that encodes both preceding and succeeding context for sequence processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626602288681ee562ad75771b687c6be</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feedforward Neural Network">
      <data key="d0">Feedforward Neural Network</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A standard neural network architecture lacking cyclic connections, contrasted with recurrent networks for modeling sequences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626602288681ee562ad75771b687c6be</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Softmax Layer">
      <data key="d0">Softmax Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A neural network activation layer that normalizes output vectors into categorical probability distributions for classification tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;An output layer configuration used in supervised neural networks with cross-entropy or hinge loss to select among ambiguous senses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d90ca4cd35ecf011b4a784edca91c3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626602288681ee562ad75771b687c6be</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sigmoid Layer">
      <data key="d0">Sigmoid Layer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An activation layer placed at the top of the proposed network architecture to evaluate whether a sense fits a given context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-626602288681ee562ad75771b687c6be</data>
    </node>
    <node id="One-Hot Representation">
      <data key="d0">One-Hot Representation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sparse vector representation of words where a single element is set to one and the remainder are zeros, used as a conventional input representation for neural networks.&lt;SEP&gt;A vector representation consisting of all zeros except for a single one, used to index specific senses or context words from embedding lookup tables.&lt;SEP&gt;One-hot representation is a sparse binary vector encoding where a single element indicates the presence of a specific word from the vocabulary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2fd39034576eb8de5d243305db77f4b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-626602288681ee562ad75771b687c6be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8ad7bf1880d82f40811c2078046e6b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WordNet 1.7.1">
      <data key="d0">WordNet 1.7.1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A lexical database and sense inventory used for annotating nouns and adjectives in the SensEval-3 dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2a718261409ed817eb2c8548c15249f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wordsmyth">
      <data key="d0">Wordsmyth</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An educational dictionary and sense inventory used for annotating verbs in the SensEval-3 dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2a718261409ed817eb2c8548c15249f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multi-classifier BLSTM">
      <data key="d0">Multi-classifier BLSTM</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A supervised word sense disambiguation model consisting of deep neural networks utilizing pre-trained word embeddings and multiple classifiers per ambiguous word.&lt;SEP&gt;Multi-classifier BLSTM is a WSD model composed of deep neural networks sharing lower layers while using separate upper layers to classify specific ambiguous words.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2a718261409ed817eb2c8548c15249f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMS+adapted CW">
      <data key="d0">IMS+adapted CW</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A supervised word sense disambiguation model based on deep neural networks and pre-trained word embeddings.&lt;SEP&gt;IMS+adapted CW is a supervised Word Sense Disambiguation model that uses deep neural networks along with pre-trained word embeddings, POS tags, and collocations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2a718261409ed817eb2c8548c15249f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLSTM">
      <data key="d0">BLSTM</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bidirectional recurrent neural network architecture consisting of left- and right-traversing LSTMs used to encode context for word sense disambiguation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BLSTM) is a neural network architecture capable of capturing sequential context from both forward and backward directions, used here for WSD.&lt;SEP&gt;Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BLSTM) is a recurrent neural network architecture used as a single-classifier model for word sense disambiguation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2a718261409ed817eb2c8548c15249f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RMSprop">
      <data key="d0">RMSprop</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An optimization algorithm used to update model parameters and weights during neural network training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2a718261409ed817eb2c8548c15249f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dropout">
      <data key="d0">Dropout</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A neural network regularization technique in which randomly selected units are omitted during training to reduce overfitting.&lt;SEP&gt;A regularization method applied with a probability of 0.2 during the training of the neural language model.&lt;SEP&gt;A regularization technique for neural networks that randomly omits hidden units during training forward-backpropagation to prevent overfitting.&lt;SEP&gt;Dropout is a regularization technique in neural network training that randomly deactivates hidden units to prevent co-adaptation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c17cbdd46b3ef569862a8fa188b4fab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26c91c9a531d8aea5394e79e584448ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db96a568a81e93ae0f5b620a7d452203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2a718261409ed817eb2c8548c15249f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dropword">
      <data key="d0">Dropword</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A word-level regularization technique derived from dropout where words in the input context are replaced with a designated tag during training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2a718261409ed817eb2c8548c15249f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Word Dropout">
      <data key="d0">Word Dropout</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A regularization method where input context words are set to zero during training to decrease dependency on individual words.&lt;SEP&gt;Word dropout is a regularization technique in neural network training that randomly zeroes word inputs to enhance model generalization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2a718261409ed817eb2c8548c15249f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mean Square Error">
      <data key="d0">Mean Square Error</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A loss function applied in the network's training that achieved superior performance compared to binary cross-entropy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2a718261409ed817eb2c8548c15249f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectified Linear Unit">
      <data key="d0">Rectified Linear Unit</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An activation function employed in computing the activations of the network's hidden layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2a718261409ed817eb2c8548c15249f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="htsa3">
      <data key="d0">htsa3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">htsa3 is a Naive Bayes-based WSD system applied to raw words, lemmas, and POS tags that won the SensEval-3 lexical sample evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IRST-Kernels">
      <data key="d0">IRST-Kernels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IRST-Kernels is a WSD system that applies kernel methods and SVM classifiers using syntagmatic information and proximity on the British National Corpus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="nusels">
      <data key="d0">nusels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">nusels is a supervised WSD system that relies on SVM classifiers combined with contextual, local, and syntactic knowledge sources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Naive Bayes">
      <data key="d0">Naive Bayes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A probabilistic classification method evaluated alongside other supervised algorithms for speech act prediction.&lt;SEP&gt;A supervised machine learning classification algorithm implemented as a reference baseline against the Support Vector Machine models.&lt;SEP&gt;Naive Bayes is a family of probabilistic classifiers based on Bayes' theorem, utilized by the htsa3 system for lexical sample disambiguation.&lt;SEP&gt;Naive Bayes is a probabilistic classifier based on Bayes' theorem applied in cuisine modeling and classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Naive Bayes is a probabilistic classifier examined by S Jayaraman et al. under unsupervised learning configurations on cuisine datasets.&lt;SEP&gt;Naive Bayes is a probabilistic machine learning algorithm frequently tested and used in sentiment classification of tweets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b6698485050fd363980b75ae23a237b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3194fa4581f2aaec823530e030775b2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e9f52662c01528fd8d607b1462c48df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc715f7f0042435219a9937b5527ecef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02b1ce0e6302d1e78683fc67d2a9ca7b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Softmax">
      <data key="d0">Softmax</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Softmax is a mathematical activation function used in the final output layer of neural networks to generate probability distributions over target classes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sense Embedding">
      <data key="d0">Sense Embedding</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sense embedding is a dense vector representation specifically assigned to individual meanings or definitions of words rather than word surface forms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supervised Learning">
      <data key="d0">Supervised Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Supervised learning is a machine learning paradigm trained on labeled data, forming the basis of several evaluated WSD systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unsupervised Methods">
      <data key="d0">Unsupervised Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Unsupervised methods are algorithmic approaches that do not rely on labeled training examples, shown in the text to occasionally outperform low-ranking supervised models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17dc83295bc11d415f3f0d85e84a5a1c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Library of Medicine">
      <data key="d0">National Library of Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Library of Medicine (NLM) is a United States government biomedical library and research organization that prepared the MSH WSD dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ding">
      <data key="d0">Ding</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A researcher associated with event-driven stock prediction methods including structured triples, NTN, and KGEB models.&lt;SEP&gt;Ding is a researcher who proposed deep event embedding models and knowledge graph integrations for stock market prediction.&lt;SEP&gt;Ding is a researcher who proposed the use of neural tensor networks for semantic composition of event arguments in 2015.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9d3fbec1ae6ff5c0cac8214c21ddda4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c25c713c59d89b362e411f24495aab21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weber">
      <data key="d0">Weber</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Weber is a researcher who proposed tensor-based composition models for event representation and developed hard similarity datasets.&lt;SEP&gt;Weber is a researcher who proposed using neural tensor networks for event argument composition and representation learning in 2018.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9d3fbec1ae6ff5c0cac8214c21ddda4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Event Representation Learning">
      <data key="d0">Event Representation Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Event Representation Learning is an artificial intelligence domain focused on mapping structured events from text into distributed vector representations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Script Event Prediction">
      <data key="d0">Script Event Prediction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A commonsense reasoning task category where a model must choose the most reasonable subsequent event from candidate options.&lt;SEP&gt;A downstream natural language processing evaluation task focused on predicting subsequent events in a narrative script.&lt;SEP&gt;An NLP benchmark task focused on predicting the next likely event in a stereotypical sequence or script.&lt;SEP&gt;Script Event Prediction is a downstream natural language processing task that predicts subsequent events based on provided contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;Script Event Prediction is an evaluation task category used to benchmark the performance of event embedding models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8fecfb2a0e52c8eb6b888c9cbd44d9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e48f19d4562e9a96fc6f5a1a6f337890&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9d3fbec1ae6ff5c0cac8214c21ddda4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c25c713c59d89b362e411f24495aab21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stock Market Prediction">
      <data key="d0">Stock Market Prediction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A financial forecasting application task that utilizes event representations to predict market movements.&lt;SEP&gt;A task category and application domain aimed at forecasting stock price trends and market volatility driven by news events.&lt;SEP&gt;Stock Market Prediction is a downstream financial application used to evaluate the utility of event embeddings in forecasting market volatilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e48f19d4562e9a96fc6f5a1a6f337890&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c25c713c59d89b362e411f24495aab21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Event Similarity">
      <data key="d0">Event Similarity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An evaluation task assessing the semantic closeness of event representations in a vector space.&lt;SEP&gt;Event Similarity is an evaluation task assessing how well learned event embeddings capture semantic relationships and distinctions between event pairs.&lt;SEP&gt;Event Similarity is an evaluation task category measuring the semantic closeness and distance between different event representations in vector space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8fecfb2a0e52c8eb6b888c9cbd44d9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9d3fbec1ae6ff5c0cac8214c21ddda4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Artificial Intelligence">
      <data key="d0">Artificial Intelligence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A broad branch of computer science focused on building intelligent systems capable of performing tasks that traditionally require human intelligence without explicit programming.&lt;SEP&gt;A field of computer science and technology aimed at creating intelligent systems capable of human-like conversation and understanding.&lt;SEP&gt;Artificial Intelligence is the broader field of computer science for which structuralizing and representing world events as machine-readable knowledge is crucial.&lt;SEP&gt;The branch of computer science focused on building intelligent machines capable of human-like conversation and problem solving.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90319301f543fcb7182148725008750f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c53d0ac7fa47d30ce6f399d3ef8b95e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18770431e1f17cd9efc7aedcdb98d303&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Commonsense Knowledge">
      <data key="d0">Commonsense Knowledge</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Commonsense knowledge encompasses external information such as participant intent and sentiment integrated into event representations to improve downstream NLP performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Commonsense knowledge encompasses understanding of human mental states, intents, and sentiments that are implicit and not directly stated in text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9d3fbec1ae6ff5c0cac8214c21ddda4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neural Tensor Network">
      <data key="d0">Neural Tensor Network</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A deep learning model architecture that uses bilinear tensors to explicitly model relational interactions among event components.&lt;SEP&gt;A neural network architecture that combines arguments multiplicatively to generate dense compositional event representations.&lt;SEP&gt;Neural Tensor Network is a family of baseline models that use tensors to learn interactions between predicates and their arguments.&lt;SEP&gt;Neural tensor network is a deep learning architecture used to perform semantic composition and capture interactions between event arguments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8fecfb2a0e52c8eb6b888c9cbd44d9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9d3fbec1ae6ff5c0cac8214c21ddda4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e48f19d4562e9a96fc6f5a1a6f337890&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parameterized Additive Models">
      <data key="d0">Parameterized Additive Models</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A baseline class of compositional models, including Comp. NN and EM Comp., compared against tensor-based models.&lt;SEP&gt;Parameterized additive models are vector space modeling methods that pass concatenated or summed word embeddings through parameterized functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c25c713c59d89b362e411f24495aab21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Event2Mind">
      <data key="d0">Event2Mind</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Event2Mind is a commonsense knowledge base that stores information regarding the intents and emotions of event participants.&lt;SEP&gt;Event2Mind is a dataset containing event phrases with placeholders such as PersonX and wildcards, used to formulate human activity search queries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a22507f0961c9cfd8c5355551b99d5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATOMIC">
      <data key="d0">ATOMIC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A commonsense knowledge dataset of 25,000 event phrases covering everyday events and annotating the intents and sentiment of event participants.&lt;SEP&gt;ATOMIC is an external commonsense knowledge base modeling everyday reasoning, participant intent, and event consequences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02f0f311662379cfa30789a001aeb981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Terrorist Attack">
      <data key="d0">Terrorist Attack</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A terrorist attack is an illustrative event scenario discussed where participants share specific intents such as causing bloodshed.&lt;SEP&gt;A violent event cited as the underlying intent or nature of someone throwing a bomb.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02f0f311662379cfa30789a001aeb981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27fdb62d78b19f761a3c7ed3273386ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Event Embedding">
      <data key="d0">Event Embedding</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A representation learning technique that maps event tuples consisting of actors, predicates, and objects into low-dimensional dense vectors.&lt;SEP&gt;Event embedding refers to the representation of structured events as dense vectors in natural language processing, used for tasks like narrative prediction and financial forecasting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e48f19d4562e9a96fc6f5a1a6f337890&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9d3fbec1ae6ff5c0cac8214c21ddda4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External Commonsense Knowledge">
      <data key="d0">External Commonsense Knowledge</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of supplementary background information, including intent and sentiment, integrated to improve the quality of event representations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e48f19d4562e9a96fc6f5a1a6f337890</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-Rank Tensor Decomposition">
      <data key="d0">Low-Rank Tensor Decomposition</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tensor compression technique that approximates higher-order tensors using low-rank matrices to mitigate the curse of dimensionality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e48f19d4562e9a96fc6f5a1a6f337890</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intent Information">
      <data key="d0">Intent Information</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of external commonsense knowledge representing the intentions and motivations behind actions within an event.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e48f19d4562e9a96fc6f5a1a6f337890</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentiment Information">
      <data key="d0">Sentiment Information</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An affective dimension of commonsense knowledge that captures the emotional tone and sentiment associated with events.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e48f19d4562e9a96fc6f5a1a6f337890</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Event Tuple">
      <data key="d0">Event Tuple</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A structured triplet representation of an event composed of an actor/subject, an action/predicate, and an object.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e48f19d4562e9a96fc6f5a1a6f337890</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Margin Loss">
      <data key="d0">Margin Loss</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An objective loss function designed to maximize the score margin between valid event tuples and randomly corrupted event tuples during training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e48f19d4562e9a96fc6f5a1a6f337890</data>
    </node>
    <node id="L2 Regularization">
      <data key="d0">L2 Regularization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A standard mathematical regularization technique with penalty weight lambda set to 0.0001, used during model training to prevent overfitting.&lt;SEP&gt;A standard regularization method evaluated in the study as an alternative to parameter freezing during child model training.&lt;SEP&gt;A weight regularization technique applied to the model parameters to prevent overfitting during event embedding training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02f0f311662379cfa30789a001aeb981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-762be16cab89cb90aaf53d4505452bf5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e48f19d4562e9a96fc6f5a1a6f337890</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hard Similarity Dataset">
      <data key="d0">Hard Similarity Dataset</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A set of benchmark evaluation datasets, split into small and big subsets, used to measure the quality and semantic similarity of event embeddings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e48f19d4562e9a96fc6f5a1a6f337890</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Commonsense Knowledge Enhanced Event Representations">
      <data key="d0">Commonsense Knowledge Enhanced Event Representations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A research framework and methodology focusing on augmenting event vector representations with external commonsense knowledge, specifically intent and sentiment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02f0f311662379cfa30789a001aeb981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intent Embedding">
      <data key="d0">Intent Embedding</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A representation learning approach that encodes the intents of event participants into event vectors to explain why an actor performed an action.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02f0f311662379cfa30789a001aeb981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentiment Embedding">
      <data key="d0">Sentiment Embedding</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A representation learning approach that encodes the emotions of event participants into event vectors to explain how the actor feels after an event.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02f0f311662379cfa30789a001aeb981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SenticNet">
      <data key="d0">SenticNet</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A knowledge base and tool used to normalize emotion words into positive or negative sentiment polarities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-02f0f311662379cfa30789a001aeb981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sap">
