    <node id="ResNet">
      <data key="d0">ResNet</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A residual neural network architecture integrated into multi-head self-attention layers to mitigate gradient vanishing and exploding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-57acb1a29c2b86c001f999352f15c3cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ResNet Architecture">
      <data key="d0">ResNet Architecture</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ResNet architecture is a deep neural network design applied within the multi-head self-attention layers to prevent gradient vanishing and exploding problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8273db0372f06b5474e49c6b558bba7b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NER">
      <data key="d0">NER</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NER (Named Entity Recognition) is a category of downstream natural language processing task focused on identifying entities by assigning sequence tags to words.&lt;SEP&gt;NER (Named Entity Recognition) is a downstream natural language processing task jointly learned with RC using the focused attention model.&lt;SEP&gt;NER (Named Entity Recognition) is a natural language processing task that identifies and classifies named entities in unstructured text.&lt;SEP&gt;Named Entity Recognition (NER) is a natural language processing task focused on identifying and classifying key entities within text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa612722a8775613a3ecb4a189613e3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e059280feab7e800ee2d2e025bc7cbd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8273db0372f06b5474e49c6b558bba7b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RC">
      <data key="d0">RC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RC (Relation Classification) is a category of downstream natural language processing task designed to determine and summarize the relation between pairs of entities.&lt;SEP&gt;RC (Relation Classification) is a downstream natural language processing task integrated into the focused attention model alongside NER.&lt;SEP&gt;RC (Relation Classification) is a natural language processing task focused on identifying and classifying semantic relationships between entity pairs.&lt;SEP&gt;Relation Classification (RC) is an information extraction task aimed at determining semantic relationships between entity pairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa612722a8775613a3ecb4a189613e3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e059280feab7e800ee2d2e025bc7cbd4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8273db0372f06b5474e49c6b558bba7b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multi-Head Self-Attention">
      <data key="d0">Multi-Head Self-Attention</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Multi-head self-attention (MHSA) is a neural network mechanism that computes context-dependent word representations using multiple parallel attention heads.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8273db0372f06b5474e49c6b558bba7b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SA-Encoder">
      <data key="d0">SA-Encoder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SA-encoder is a self-attention encoder that produces context-dependent word representations from an input sequence and a MASK matrix before passing them to downstream task layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8273db0372f06b5474e49c6b558bba7b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MASK Matrix">
      <data key="d0">MASK Matrix</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MASK matrix is a parameter matrix of size T x T used to control the range of attention across word positions in self-attention mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8273db0372f06b5474e49c6b558bba7b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLS Token">
      <data key="d0">CLS Token</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">[CLS] is a special classification token placed at the beginning of an input sequence, adopted from BERT.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8273db0372f06b5474e49c6b558bba7b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEP Token">
      <data key="d0">SEP Token</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">[SEP] is a special separator token appended to the end of an input sequence to denote termination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8273db0372f06b5474e49c6b558bba7b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIEOS Tag">
      <data key="d0">BIEOS Tag</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BIEOS tag represents a category of sequence labeling scheme utilized in named entity recognition to identify the boundaries and types of entities in a text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e059280feab7e800ee2d2e025bc7cbd4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Text">
      <data key="d0">Medical Text</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical text represents a domain-specific category of textual data referenced for applying and analyzing the relation classification task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e059280feab7e800ee2d2e025bc7cbd4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRF Model">
      <data key="d0">CRF Model</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A CRF (Conditional Random Field) model is a category of statistical modeling method used in the NER downstream task layer to capture transition and emission probabilities between sequential tags.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e059280feab7e800ee2d2e025bc7cbd4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pre-Trained Language Model">
      <data key="d0">Pre-Trained Language Model</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A pre-trained language model is a category of deep learning model trained on large-scale text corpora that contains prior linguistic knowledge used for transfer learning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e059280feab7e800ee2d2e025bc7cbd4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Single-Layer Neural Network">
      <data key="d0">Single-Layer Neural Network</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A single-layer neural network is a category of baseline neural architecture contrasted with CRF models due to its limitation in capturing link relations between adjacent tags.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e059280feab7e800ee2d2e025bc7cbd4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shanghai Shuguang Hospital">
      <data key="d0">Shanghai Shuguang Hospital</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Shanghai Shuguang Hospital is a medical institution from which coronary arteriography reports were collected to create an entity and relation extraction dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shanghai">
      <data key="d0">Shanghai</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Shanghai is a major metropolitan city in China where Shanghai Shuguang Hospital is located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coronary Arteriography Reports">
      <data key="d0">Coronary Arteriography Reports</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Coronary Arteriography Reports are clinical diagnostic documents from which entities and relations were extracted for the experimental dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Negation">
      <data key="d0">Negation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Negation is an entity category defined in the medical dataset indicating the absence or denial of a clinical condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Body Part">
      <data key="d0">Body Part</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Body Part is an entity category defined in the medical dataset identifying anatomical structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Degree">
      <data key="d0">Degree</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Degree is an entity category defined in the medical dataset describing the severity or extent of a finding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quantifier">
      <data key="d0">Quantifier</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Quantifier is an entity category defined in the medical dataset specifying numerical values or quantitative descriptors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Location">
      <data key="d0">Location</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A named entity category designating geographical places and sites, accounting for 282 annotations in the analyzed Turkish tweet dataset.&lt;SEP&gt;Location is an entity category defined in the medical dataset indicating positional or spatial references in medical text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-731ed10b120fb04a1b73bc728f12beb5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Negative">
      <data key="d0">Negative</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Negative is a relation category identified in the medical dataset connecting entities with negation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modifier">
      <data key="d0">Modifier</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Modifier is a relation category identified in the medical dataset linking descriptive modifiers to entities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Position">
      <data key="d0">Position</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Position is a relation category identified in the medical dataset representing location or directional associations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Percentage">
      <data key="d0">Percentage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Percentage is a relation category identified in the medical dataset representing proportion or percentage relations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="No Relation">
      <data key="d0">No Relation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">No Relation is a category denoting the absence of a meaningful relationship between entity pairs in the dataset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Joint Entity And Relation Extraction">
      <data key="d0">Joint Entity And Relation Extraction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Joint Entity and Relation Extraction is a sub-task and research problem in natural language processing aimed at simultaneously identifying entities and their semantic relations.&lt;SEP&gt;Joint Entity and Relation Extraction is an integrated NLP task that simultaneously identifies entities and their semantic relations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa612722a8775613a3ecb4a189613e3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adam Optimization Algorithm">
      <data key="d0">Adam Optimization Algorithm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adam is an optimization algorithm used to train and update parameters in the neural network model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Joint-Bi-LSTM">
      <data key="d0">Joint-Bi-LSTM</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A baseline neural network model architecture used for comparison in the task of joint entity and relation extraction.&lt;SEP&gt;Joint-Bi-LSTM is a generic domain baseline model used for comparison in joint entity and relation extraction tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1fb2eeb8c8333a70050efefb9412104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RDCNN">
      <data key="d0">RDCNN</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RDCNN is a medical domain neural network model used as a baseline for named entity recognition evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RCN">
      <data key="d0">RCN</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RCN is a medical domain baseline neural model evaluated specifically on relation classification tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Precision">
      <data key="d0">Precision</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Precision is an evaluation metric measuring the proportion of true positive predictions among all positive instances identified.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recall">
      <data key="d0">Recall</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Recall is an evaluation metric measuring the proportion of actual positive instances successfully retrieved by the model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="F1-Score">
      <data key="d0">F1-Score</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">F1-score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, serving as a primary overall performance metric for NER, RC, and joint models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b74404709f6b49b2bef33ca7f921e55</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Key R&amp;D Program of China">
      <data key="d0">National Key R&amp;D Program of China</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A government research funding initiative in China that supported the research under the Precision Medical Research grant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1fb2eeb8c8333a70050efefb9412104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="STR-encoder">
      <data key="d0">STR-encoder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A shared task representation encoder transformed from BERT using a dynamic range attention mechanism to generate strong feature representations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1fb2eeb8c8333a70050efefb9412104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coronary Angiography Texts">
      <data key="d0">Coronary Angiography Texts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A clinical domain dataset of medical texts used to verify the effectiveness and performance of the proposed extraction model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1fb2eeb8c8333a70050efefb9412104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Precision Medical Research">
      <data key="d0">Precision Medical Research</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A national scientific research initiative and grant focus area aimed at medical text structuring and advanced healthcare intelligence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1fb2eeb8c8333a70050efefb9412104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bigram Frequencies Calculation">
      <data key="d0">Bigram Frequencies Calculation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A computational natural language processing task discussed for its efficiency and suitability when applied to short text corpora.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1fb2eeb8c8333a70050efefb9412104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anonymous Reviewers">
      <data key="d0">Anonymous Reviewers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Peer reviewers who evaluated the manuscript and provided valuable feedback and comments acknowledged by the authors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1fb2eeb8c8333a70050efefb9412104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Editors">
      <data key="d0">Editors</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Editorial personnel acknowledged for coordinating the evaluation and publication of the research paper.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1fb2eeb8c8333a70050efefb9412104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Joel Nothman">
      <data key="d0">Joel Nothman</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Joel Nothman is a researcher or practitioner acknowledged for having a conversation with the authors and sketching a solution for calculating bigram frequencies in short texts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa612722a8775613a3ecb4a189613e3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Church">
      <data key="d0">Church</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Church is a researcher and co-author of a widely cited paper with Hanks proposing an approximation method for calculating bigram frequencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa612722a8775613a3ecb4a189613e3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hanks">
      <data key="d0">Hanks</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Hanks is a researcher and co-author with Church of a seminal paper on word association norms and bigram frequency approximations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa612722a8775613a3ecb4a189613e3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bilingual Lexicon Induction">
      <data key="d0">Bilingual Lexicon Induction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A natural language processing task (abbreviated as BLI) focused on mapping and translating lexical and morphological forms across languages.&lt;SEP&gt;An established computational linguistics task focused on inducing word translations and serving as a standard evaluation benchmark for cross-lingual word embedding models.&lt;SEP&gt;Bilingual Lexicon Induction (BLI) is a computational linguistics task that aims to induce cross-lingual word mappings and dictionaries from monolingual embeddings.&lt;SEP&gt;Bilingual Lexicon Induction (BLI) is a computational linguistics task that focuses on inducing translations between word forms across different languages.&lt;SEP&gt;Bilingual Lexicon Induction (BLI) is a natural language processing task aimed at inducing word translations between language pairs, particularly focusing on generalizing to rare and novel words.&lt;SEP&gt;Bilingual Lexicon Induction (BLI) is a task in natural language processing aimed at learning word translations between languages.&lt;SEP&gt;Bilingual Lexicon Induction (BLI) is an NLP evaluation task mentioned in the document's introductory fragments concerning lexical paradigms.&lt;SEP&gt;Bilingual lexicon induction is a natural language processing technique for extracting translation pairs across languages using comparable corpora.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa612722a8775613a3ecb4a189613e3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ca3ab8f8c0dca0a40d79a8ee4226255&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-874a0fecf572a7885d7353d9aa510123&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22000c8a3ad4361f1298f9c99632a667&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9896f0582e2237c4a0dd8169f6817110&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00381e9f572e2f4c1655790b19b827fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a857415cf32d5d04b955b0da8d697209&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cb472a550ed02164d9c3ff8cf171759</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comparable Corpora">
      <data key="d0">Comparable Corpora</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Comparable Corpora are collections of multilingual documents that share topical alignment but lack direct sentence-by-sentence alignment.&lt;SEP&gt;Comparable corpora are collections of texts in multiple languages that discuss similar topics without being direct sentence-by-sentence translations of each other.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-116526d304daa442dbc1df519e7f8fc5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa612722a8775613a3ecb4a189613e3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Siamese Bidirectional Recurrent Neural Network">
      <data key="d0">Siamese Bidirectional Recurrent Neural Network</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Siamese bidirectional recurrent neural network is a deep learning architecture used to identify and extract parallel sentences from comparable multilingual articles.&lt;SEP&gt;A deep learning architecture featuring two identical bidirectional recurrent neural networks used to extract parallel sentences from multilingual comparable corpora without requiring pre-existing translation systems or extensive feature engineering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-116526d304daa442dbc1df519e7f8fc5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa612722a8775613a3ecb4a189613e3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bigram Frequency Calculation">
      <data key="d0">Bigram Frequency Calculation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bigram frequency calculation is a statistical natural language processing task that computes the occurrence of word pairs within specified window sizes across text corpora.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa612722a8775613a3ecb4a189613e3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parallel Sentence Extraction">
      <data key="d0">Parallel Sentence Extraction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Parallel Sentence Extraction is the methodology and pipeline used to identify and decode translation-equivalent sentence pairs from comparable bilingual text.&lt;SEP&gt;Parallel Sentence Extraction is the technique of identifying and extracting translation-equivalent sentence pairs from non-parallel or comparable texts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2af0dac5bc5afdea73673fd2462c1e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-116526d304daa442dbc1df519e7f8fc5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maximum Entropy Classifier">
      <data key="d0">Maximum Entropy Classifier</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A statistical machine learning model utilized in prior alignment methods to evaluate and score sentence pairs extracted from comparable corpora based on surface features and word overlap.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-116526d304daa442dbc1df519e7f8fc5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bilingual Lexicon">
      <data key="d0">Bilingual Lexicon</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A cross-lingual dictionary or mapping between words in two languages, induced from comparable corpora to alleviate out-of-vocabulary challenges in translation systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-116526d304daa442dbc1df519e7f8fc5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Out-Of-Vocabulary">
      <data key="d0">Out-Of-Vocabulary</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A machine translation challenge where words appearing in source documents are absent from training corpora, resulting in untranslated tokens and reduced translation quality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-116526d304daa442dbc1df519e7f8fc5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Workshops on Machine Translation">
      <data key="d0">Workshops on Machine Translation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Collaborative academic gatherings and shared evaluation tasks that provide crowd-sourced parallel datasets to advance translation research on various language pairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-116526d304daa442dbc1df519e7f8fc5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lucene">
      <data key="d0">Lucene</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An open-source search engine software library whose Levenshtein distance implementation and indexing capabilities were utilized for string matching and token resolution.&lt;SEP&gt;An open-source text search engine software library used to execute information retrieval queries over entire comparable corpora based on characteristic domain vocabularies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0d30e05ac4755125277651156c72d63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-116526d304daa442dbc1df519e7f8fc5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wikimedia">
      <data key="d0">Wikimedia</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Wikimedia is the organization that hosts Wikipedia and provides the data dumps utilized to collect bilingual English-Tamil and English-Hindi articles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2af0dac5bc5afdea73673fd2462c1e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Moses">
      <data key="d0">Moses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Moses is an open-source statistical machine translation system and toolkit that includes scripts like multi-bleu for model evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;Moses is an open-source statistical machine translation toolkit and research community project used for training phrase-based translation models and evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2af0dac5bc5afdea73673fd2462c1e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fff775482c4d8a3088b428b10d9f761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Siamese Network">
      <data key="d0">Siamese Network</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Siamese Network is an artificial neural network architecture using shared or twin sub-networks to map candidate sentence pairs into continuous representations for similarity estimation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2af0dac5bc5afdea73673fd2462c1e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bidirectional RNN">
      <data key="d0">Bidirectional RNN</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bidirectional RNN is a recurrent neural network architecture that processes text sequences in both forward and backward directions to encode sentence representations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2af0dac5bc5afdea73673fd2462c1e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence-to-Sequence">
      <data key="d0">Sequence-to-Sequence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sequence-to-sequence (Seq2seq) is a neural architecture paradigm that maps an input sequence to an output sequence, commonly used in machine translation.&lt;SEP&gt;Sequence-to-sequence is a neural network architecture paradigm that maps an input sequence to an output sequence, widely used in translation and question answering.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2af0dac5bc5afdea73673fd2462c1e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fff775482c4d8a3088b428b10d9f761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Negative Sampling">
      <data key="d0">Negative Sampling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Negative Sampling is a training method in machine learning where non-parallel sentence pairs are generated randomly to teach classifiers to discriminate non-translations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2af0dac5bc5afdea73673fd2462c1e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greedy Decoding">
      <data key="d0">Greedy Decoding</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Greedy Decoding is a sequence inference strategy used during decoding and sentence alignment to extract high-precision parallel sentences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2af0dac5bc5afdea73673fd2462c1e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Workshop on Machine Translation">
      <data key="d0">Workshop on Machine Translation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Workshop on Machine Translation (WMT) is an academic conference event featuring shared tasks such as the Parallel Corpus Filtering task in its 2018 edition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fff775482c4d8a3088b428b10d9f761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parallel Corpus Filtering">
      <data key="d0">Parallel Corpus Filtering</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Parallel Corpus Filtering is a competitive shared task introduced at the 2018 Workshop on Machine Translation (WMT) focused on filtering noisy web-crawled text into high-quality parallel sentence pairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fff775482c4d8a3088b428b10d9f761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="OpenNMT">
      <data key="d0">OpenNMT</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">OpenNMT is an open-source neural machine translation framework and research ecosystem utilized for training sequence-to-sequence and transformer models.&lt;SEP&gt;OpenNMT is an open-source project and generic deep learning framework specialized in sequence-to-sequence neural network models for NLP tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea9c29cc7a5d0f200ae552bfc4494ad2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fff775482c4d8a3088b428b10d9f761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TensorFlow">
      <data key="d0">TensorFlow</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">TensorFlow is an open-source machine learning and deep learning framework developed by Google used here to implement OpenNMT models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fff775482c4d8a3088b428b10d9f761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="English-Tamil">
      <data key="d0">English-Tamil</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">English-Tamil (en-ta) is a low-resource language pair evaluated in the parallel sentence extraction and translation experiments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fff775482c4d8a3088b428b10d9f761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="English-Hindi">
      <data key="d0">English-Hindi</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">English-Hindi (en-hi) is a language pair evaluated in the translation experiments to assess BLEU score improvements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fff775482c4d8a3088b428b10d9f761</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ConceptNet">
      <data key="d0">ConceptNet</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">ConceptNet is a semantic knowledge graph project used to identify and expand relationships between words and entities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb73055a3a263fcef16382513853f022</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Extractive Question Answering">
      <data key="d0">Extractive Question Answering</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A task within question answering where the answer is directly extracted as a segment or span from the source context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb73055a3a263fcef16382513853f022</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FastQA">
      <data key="d0">FastQA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An RNN-based baseline neural system designed for extractive question answering that incorporates question-word awareness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb73055a3a263fcef16382513853f022</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gated-Attention Reader">
      <data key="d0">Gated-Attention Reader</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A multi-hop neural architecture featuring a multiplicative gating mechanism designed for answering cloze-style questions over documents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb73055a3a263fcef16382513853f022</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dependency Parsing">
      <data key="d0">Dependency Parsing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A natural language processing technique that analyzes grammatical structure to determine relationships between words in a question or sentence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb73055a3a263fcef16382513853f022</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conversational Modelling">
      <data key="d0">Conversational Modelling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The subfield of NLP and AI focused on generating multi-turn dialogues and responses for conversational agents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb73055a3a263fcef16382513853f022</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Summarisation">
      <data key="d0">Summarisation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Summarisation is a natural language processing task focused on producing a concise and fluent summary of a longer text document.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea9c29cc7a5d0f200ae552bfc4494ad2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Speech Processing">
      <data key="d0">Speech Processing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Speech processing is an area of computer science and signal processing dealing with the analysis, synthesis, and recognition of spoken language.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea9c29cc7a5d0f200ae552bfc4494ad2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Character-Based Neural Model">
      <data key="d0">Character-Based Neural Model</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Character-based neural models operate on individual characters rather than whole words to bypass vocabulary limitations and handle out-of-vocabulary tokens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea9c29cc7a5d0f200ae552bfc4494ad2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="N-Triples">
      <data key="d0">N-Triples</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">N-Triples is a line-based, plain text format for encoding Resource Description Framework (RDF) graphs, representing subject-predicate-object knowledge facts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea9c29cc7a5d0f200ae552bfc4494ad2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DBpedia Infobox Properties">
      <data key="d0">DBpedia Infobox Properties</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DBpedia Infobox Properties is a structured knowledge dataset extracted from Wikipedia infoboxes, providing semantic property triples used to form question-answer pairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea9c29cc7a5d0f200ae552bfc4494ad2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METEOR">
      <data key="d0">METEOR</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">METEOR is an evaluation metric based on the harmonic mean of precision and recall that incorporates stemming, paraphrasing, and synonymy matching.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea9c29cc7a5d0f200ae552bfc4494ad2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chrF">
      <data key="d0">chrF</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">chrF is a character n-gram F-score evaluation metric used to assess generated text quality without heavily penalizing differences in word inflections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea9c29cc7a5d0f200ae552bfc4494ad2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QA System Experiment">
      <data key="d0">QA System Experiment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The QA system experiment is the scientific evaluation and empirical training conducted to test seq2seq neural networks on question-answer pairs extracted from DBpedia and subtitles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea9c29cc7a5d0f200ae552bfc4494ad2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Science Foundation Ireland">
      <data key="d0">Science Foundation Ireland</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A statutory body in Ireland that provides financial support for scientific research, funding the presented QA research under Grant Number SFI/12/RC/2289.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insight">
      <data key="d0">Insight</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A research centre associated with Science Foundation Ireland involved in the funded research project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jean Michel Diot">
      <data key="d0">Jean Michel Diot</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An individual mentioned in an evaluation example regarding a question about his nationality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clark Duke">
      <data key="d0">Clark Duke</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An individual mentioned in an evaluation example where the neural network incorrectly identified him as a football player.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Edith Roger">
      <data key="d0">Edith Roger</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A person mentioned in an evaluation query whose birthplace is Vestby, Norway, but was misidentified by the neural network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aristotle">
      <data key="d0">Aristotle</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A historical figure used as an example of DBpedia n-triples converted into aligned data format.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vestby">
      <data key="d0">Vestby</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A municipality in Norway, noted as the actual birthplace of Edith Roger.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Norway">
      <data key="d0">Norway</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A country in northern Europe, the true country of birth for Edith Roger.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vienna">
      <data key="d0">Vienna</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The capital of Austria, incorrectly generated by the neural network as the birthplace of Edith Roger.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Austria">
      <data key="d0">Austria</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A country in Central Europe, incorrectly predicted as the country of birth for Edith Roger.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Question Answering System">
      <data key="d0">Question Answering System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A domain of artificial intelligence systems designed to automatically answer questions asked by humans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sequence-to-Sequence Neural Model">
      <data key="d0">Sequence-to-Sequence Neural Model</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A deep learning architecture category used for mapping sequences to sequences, applied to question answering and dialogue modeling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dialogue Agents">
      <data key="d0">Dialogue Agents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intelligent conversational agents designed to produce natural responses in human-computer interaction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imagine-Then-Arbitrate Model">
      <data key="d0">Imagine-Then-Arbitrate Model</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A neural dialogue model architecture designed to predict conversational turn-taking and decide whether an agent should wait or reply.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="METEOR Metric">
      <data key="d0">METEOR Metric</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An automatic evaluation metric based on the harmonic mean of precision and recall with stemming, paraphrasing, and synonymy matching.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BLEU Metric">
      <data key="d0">BLEU Metric</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An automatic n-gram precision overlap evaluation metric used to evaluate the hypothesis answers against gold standard answers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chrF Metric">
      <data key="d0">chrF Metric</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A character n-gram evaluation metric that correlates well with human judgment, especially for morphologically rich languages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaginator Module">
      <data key="d0">Imaginator Module</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sub-component of the ITA neural dialogue model designed to learn the user's and agent's speaking styles and generate possible future utterances.&lt;SEP&gt;A submodule of the Imagine-then-Arbitrate neural dialogue system tasked with simulating conversational continuations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f148e57eed21f7948ba04d2d12e56d0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Arbitrator Module">
      <data key="d0">Arbitrator Module</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A component of the ITA model that combines conversation history with generated hypothetical utterances from imaginators to decide whether the agent should wait or reply.&lt;SEP&gt;A decision-making component of the Imagine-then-Arbitrate dialogue model that arbitrates whether to wait or generate an immediate response.&lt;SEP&gt;A text classification and response selection module designed to evaluate predicted dialogue paths and determine whether an agent should respond or wait.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f148e57eed21f7948ba04d2d12e56d0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15b5829d32b6817e2c4e142dba61455b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae11879a44e89506f5f7277c88df7c75</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imagine-then-Arbitrate">
      <data key="d0">Imagine-then-Arbitrate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A dialogue modeling framework that uses dialogue history, ground truth labels, and simulated future utterances to determine agent turn-taking actions.&lt;SEP&gt;A novel neural dialogue model framework consisting of two imaginator modules and an arbitrator module, designed to decide whether an agent should wait or reply directly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f148e57eed21f7948ba04d2d12e56d0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b6907deaed642269724a32b0c9ccd9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dialogue System">
      <data key="d0">Dialogue System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A human-computer interaction system or agent used in applications like personal assistants, customer service, and chitchat, focused on generating responses and managing conversational flow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f148e57eed21f7948ba04d2d12e56d0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seq2Seq">
      <data key="d0">Seq2Seq</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sequence-to-sequence neural model framework used in deep learning for dialogue response generation and natural language processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f148e57eed21f7948ba04d2d12e56d0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TextCNN">
      <data key="d0">TextCNN</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A convolutional neural network architecture built on pre-trained word vectors used for sentence-level text classification tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;A convolutional neural network architecture category for text processing, utilized as a base structure for the arbitrator module.&lt;SEP&gt;A convolutional neural network architecture trained on pre-trained word vectors for sentence-level classification tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f148e57eed21f7948ba04d2d12e56d0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15b5829d32b6817e2c4e142dba61455b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b6907deaed642269724a32b0c9ccd9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Task-Oriented Dialogue Models">
      <data key="d0">Task-Oriented Dialogue Models</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of dialogue systems designed to assist users in accomplishing specific tasks by incorporating external knowledge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f148e57eed21f7948ba04d2d12e56d0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Personal Assistant">
      <data key="d0">Personal Assistant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A commercial application category of dialogue systems providing automated conversational assistance to users.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f148e57eed21f7948ba04d2d12e56d0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intelligent Customer Service">
      <data key="d0">Intelligent Customer Service</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An automated business application of dialogue agents designed to interact with clients and address service inquiries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f148e57eed21f7948ba04d2d12e56d0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chitchat Companion">
      <data key="d0">Chitchat Companion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An open-domain conversational agent designed for casual social interaction and entertainment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f148e57eed21f7948ba04d2d12e56d0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emotion Recognition">
      <data key="d0">Emotion Recognition</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A classification task in dialogue systems aimed at detecting human emotional states from conversational text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f148e57eed21f7948ba04d2d12e56d0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gender Recognition">
      <data key="d0">Gender Recognition</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A classification task used within conversational analysis and natural language processing to identify speaker gender.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f148e57eed21f7948ba04d2d12e56d0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Semantically Controlled LSTM">
      <data key="d0">Semantically Controlled LSTM</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An extension of LSTM that incorporates semantic control for data-driven language generation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b6907deaed642269724a32b0c9ccd9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SEQ2SEQ">
      <data key="d0">SEQ2SEQ</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sequence generation model architecture often implemented with encoder and decoder networks for utterance generation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b6907deaed642269724a32b0c9ccd9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dialogue Manager">
      <data key="d0">Dialogue Manager</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A system component in dialogue architectures responsible for selecting communication goals to be converted into natural language by generation modules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b6907deaed642269724a32b0c9ccd9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dialogue Agent">
      <data key="d0">Dialogue Agent</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An automated conversational participant or chatbot designed to decide the appropriate timing to reply rather than responding to every message.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b6907deaed642269724a32b0c9ccd9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human User">
      <data key="d0">Human User</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A conversational participant interacting with the dialogue agent across multiple conversation turns and subturns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b6907deaed642269724a32b0c9ccd9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rule-Based Approach">
      <data key="d0">Rule-Based Approach</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A traditional NLG methodology relying on hand-crafted templates, rules, and heuristics designed by domain experts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b6907deaed642269724a32b0c9ccd9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Retrieval Method">
      <data key="d0">Retrieval Method</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A dialogue response approach that selects existing candidate responses, distinct from generative natural language generation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b6907deaed642269724a32b0c9ccd9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaginator">
      <data key="d0">Imaginator</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A generative module designed to predict potential agent and user responses based on prior dialogue history.&lt;SEP&gt;A sequence generation component within the ITA framework that learns conversational styles to simulate prospective future dialogue utterances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e97e9e342e051e03614c8dcaa59a2bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b6907deaed642269724a32b0c9ccd9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agent's Imaginator">
      <data key="d0">Agent's Imaginator</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialized imaginator module trained on conversational history to mimic the dialogue agent's speaking style and generate anticipated agent utterances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b6907deaed642269724a32b0c9ccd9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="User's Imaginator">
      <data key="d0">User's Imaginator</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialized imaginator module trained on conversational history to learn the human user's speaking style and generate anticipated user utterances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b6907deaed642269724a32b0c9ccd9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Task-Oriented Dialogue">
      <data key="d0">Task-Oriented Dialogue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of conversational systems and datasets focused on assisting users to accomplish specific tasks, exemplified by MultiWOZ 2.0 and DSTC 2.&lt;SEP&gt;A functional category of conversational AI systems and corpora oriented toward completing specific tasks using slots and ontology values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e97e9e342e051e03614c8dcaa59a2bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15b5829d32b6817e2c4e142dba61455b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chitchat">
      <data key="d0">Chitchat</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A category of informal, open-domain conversational interaction, represented in the text by datasets such as DailyDialogue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-15b5829d32b6817e2c4e142dba61455b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human-Computer Interaction">
      <data key="d0">Human-Computer Interaction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A domain and category of interactive communication between humans and computational systems that the proposed conversational approach targets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-15b5829d32b6817e2c4e142dba61455b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DSTC 2">
      <data key="d0">DSTC 2</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Dialog State Tracking Challenge 2, a recognized research evaluation competition and benchmark event for task-oriented dialogue systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The second Dialogue State Tracking Challenge, serving as an earlier benchmark and dataset for task-oriented dialogue research.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e97e9e342e051e03614c8dcaa59a2bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15b5829d32b6817e2c4e142dba61455b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bi-GRU">
      <data key="d0">Bi-GRU</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bidirectional gated recurrent unit neural network architecture category employed to implement the arbitrator module.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-15b5829d32b6817e2c4e142dba61455b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MultiWOZ 2.0">
      <data key="d0">MultiWOZ 2.0</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A large-scale multi-domain human-human written benchmark dataset representing task-oriented dialogue systems.&lt;SEP&gt;A large-scale, fully-labeled multi-domain task-oriented dialogue dataset containing human-human written conversations across several topics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e97e9e342e051e03614c8dcaa59a2bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15b5829d32b6817e2c4e142dba61455b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DailyDialogue">
      <data key="d0">DailyDialogue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A high-quality multi-turn dialogue dataset containing natural conversations about daily life, displaying complex human chatting behaviors.&lt;SEP&gt;A multi-turn conversational dataset collected from human-to-human communications, representing the chitchat dialogue category.&lt;SEP&gt;A multi-turn dialogue dataset used to benchmark dialogue generation and interaction models, modified to evaluate imaginators and arbitrators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e97e9e342e051e03614c8dcaa59a2bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15b5829d32b6817e2c4e142dba61455b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfa367d5347172364eadcba53c1dc471</data>
    </node>
    <node id="KVR">
      <data key="d0">KVR</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A task-oriented dialogue dataset used as a reference benchmark prior to MultiWOZ.&lt;SEP&gt;An annotated task-oriented dialogue corpus used for benchmarking conversational systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e97e9e342e051e03614c8dcaa59a2bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15b5829d32b6817e2c4e142dba61455b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agent Imaginator">
      <data key="d0">Agent Imaginator</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A generative sequence-to-sequence dialogue module trained on conversation history to predict future agent utterances.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific imaginator model trained to simulate and predict the speaking style and responses of the dialogue agent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e97e9e342e051e03614c8dcaa59a2bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15b5829d32b6817e2c4e142dba61455b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="User Imaginator">
      <data key="d0">User Imaginator</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A generative dialogue module trained to predict upcoming user utterances from dialogue history.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific imaginator model trained to simulate and predict the speaking style and utterances of the user.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e97e9e342e051e03614c8dcaa59a2bbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15b5829d32b6817e2c4e142dba61455b</data>
