    </edge>
    <edge source="Byte Pair Encoding" target="Sennrich">
      <data key="d4">35.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sennrich adapted Byte Pair Encoding from text compression for use in neural machine translation.&lt;SEP&gt;Sennrich first introduced the Byte Pair Encoding segmentation algorithm to the field of Neural Machine Translation.&lt;SEP&gt;Sennrich investigated merge operation hyperparameter scaling and joint bilingual subword vocabulary learning for Byte Pair Encoding.&lt;SEP&gt;Sennrich is referenced as having described the Byte Pair Encoding algorithm at length in neural machine translation literature.</data>
      <data key="d6">academic research, method development&lt;SEP&gt;algorithmic adaptation, research contribution&lt;SEP&gt;algorithmic description, scientific reference&lt;SEP&gt;authorship, academic contribution</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ba3fa25c5e29c7ea3201eac4d5fbec61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffaa7abbd8709e94a9523e6b922ac96b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acd45458d7c3c63e9891f23252405a17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e060c5bbcfa40d71aa9fb0a2e63bff30</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Byte Pair Encoding" target="Orthographic Syllables">
      <data key="d4">44.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both are evaluated and compared as subword units providing context for statistical machine translation between related languages.&lt;SEP&gt;Byte Pair Encoding is experimentally compared with orthographic syllables to determine translation accuracy and efficiency across various language pairs.&lt;SEP&gt;Byte Pair Encoding units are directly compared against orthographic syllables as subword translation representations.&lt;SEP&gt;Byte Pair Encoding units outperform Orthographic Syllable units in morphological translation, cross-domain scenarios, and adaptability to writing systems lacking explicit vowel representation.&lt;SEP&gt;Byte Pair Encoding was compared against orthographic syllables, outperforming them across translation benchmarks.</data>
