    </edge>
    <edge source="Personalized Language Models" target="Calling Speech Recognition Task">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The calling speech recognition task utilizes a personalized language model containing individual contact lists.</data>
      <data key="d6">domain application, personalization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fdb05b2a1cbb63e80256245c9da202fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Personalized Language Models" target="Dynamic WFST Decoding">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Personalized language models are integrated into dynamic WFST decoding graphs using dynamic composition and caching.</data>
      <data key="d6">model integration, decoding architecture</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3f30bf0da65382dcb7b8ad1db0f03843</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Dynamic WFST Decoder" target="Two-Layer Cache Mechanism">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The two-layer cache mechanism is integrated into dynamic WFST decoders to accelerate personalized language model processing.</data>
      <data key="d6">performance optimization, architectural integration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fdb05b2a1cbb63e80256245c9da202fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Dynamic WFST Decoder" target="Pre-Initialization Methods">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pre-initialization methods are used to prime WFST caches and reduce decoding latency in dynamic WFST decoders.</data>
      <data key="d6">optimization, efficiency enhancement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fdb05b2a1cbb63e80256245c9da202fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Dynamic WFST Decoder" target="HCLG Graph">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Dynamic WFST decoders address the computational and memory limitations associated with statically composed HCLG graphs.</data>
      <data key="d6">comparative paradigm, system improvement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fdb05b2a1cbb63e80256245c9da202fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Dynamic WFST Decoder" target="Personalized Language Model">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Dynamic WFST decoders are well-suited for supporting personalized language models via on-demand lazy composition.</data>
      <data key="d6">decoder adaptation, personalization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7994dd4a5f0c1996cedd50a0545ebc9a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Dynamic WFST Decoder" target="Statically Composed Graph">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Dynamic WFST decoders provide an alternative to statically composed graphs to overcome memory constraints and support personalization.</data>
      <data key="d6">computational efficiency, structural contrast</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7994dd4a5f0c1996cedd50a0545ebc9a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Dynamic WFST Decoder" target="Calling Domain">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The efficiency improvements of dynamic WFST decoding and pre-initialization methods are evaluated on speech recognition tasks in the calling domain.</data>
      <data key="d6">experimental evaluation, domain application</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7994dd4a5f0c1996cedd50a0545ebc9a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Dynamic WFST Decoder" target="Lazy Composition">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Dynamic WFST decoders apply lazy on-the-fly composition to merge background graphs with dynamic entities.</data>
      <data key="d6">decoding mechanism, dynamic expansion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7994dd4a5f0c1996cedd50a0545ebc9a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Dynamic WFST Decoder" target="Weighted Finite-State Transducer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Weighted finite-state transducers form the underlying mathematical and structural framework of dynamic WFST decoders.</data>
      <data key="d6">theoretical foundation, structural component</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7994dd4a5f0c1996cedd50a0545ebc9a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Two-Layer Cache Mechanism" target="Public Cache">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The public cache serves as the first layer in the proposed two-layer cache mechanism, storing the static portion of the graph.</data>
      <data key="d6">component, architecture</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fdb05b2a1cbb63e80256245c9da202fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Two-Layer Cache Mechanism" target="Private Cache">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The private cache serves as the second layer in the two-layer cache mechanism, storing user-specific personalized language model graphs.</data>
      <data key="d6">component, architecture</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fdb05b2a1cbb63e80256245c9da202fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Two-Layer Cache Mechanism" target="Calling Speech Recognition Task">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The calling speech recognition task is the benchmark used to demonstrate the decoding latency reductions achieved by the cache mechanism.</data>
      <data key="d6">evaluation, empirical testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fdb05b2a1cbb63e80256245c9da202fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Public Cache" target="Private Cache">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Public cache and private cache together constitute the two-layered cached FST architecture, separating static shared states from dynamic personalized states.</data>
      <data key="d6">caching architecture, data hierarchy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dacf09330ac9f65bece416e803b0daa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Public Cache" target="Run Time Factor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The static public cache significantly lowers the run time factor during online decoding by storing the most frequently accessed states.</data>
      <data key="d6">performance impact, latency reduction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dacf09330ac9f65bece416e803b0daa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Private Cache" target="Dialog Session">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The lifetime of the private cache spans the entire dialog session, persisting state updates across multiple utterances until the session ends.</data>
      <data key="d6">session lifecycle, persistence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dacf09330ac9f65bece416e803b0daa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Private Cache" target="Run Time Factor">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Accumulating composed states in the private cache throughout a dialog session progressively improves the run time factor for subsequent utterances.</data>
      <data key="d6">latency reduction, session optimization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dacf09330ac9f65bece416e803b0daa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pre-Initialization Methods" target="Breadth-First Search">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Breadth-first search is one of the two proposed pre-initialization methods used to explore decoder states.</data>
      <data key="d6">algorithmic method, sub-category</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fdb05b2a1cbb63e80256245c9da202fa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Breadth-First Search" target="Data-Driven Approach">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both breadth-first search and the data-driven approach are compared and combined for pre-initializing graphs without pre-calculated state frequencies.</data>
      <data key="d6">algorithmic comparison, complementary methods</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7994dd4a5f0c1996cedd50a0545ebc9a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Breadth-First Search" target="Distance-Based Method">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The distance-based pre-composition method directly uses breadth-first search to traverse and select states within a specific distance threshold.</data>
      <data key="d6">algorithmic implementation, graph traversal</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dacf09330ac9f65bece416e803b0daa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Personalized Language Model" target="Class Language Model">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A class language model acts as a generic root transducer whose class labels are replaced by personalized language models for individual users.</data>
      <data key="d6">transducer composition, entity replacement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7994dd4a5f0c1996cedd50a0545ebc9a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Personalized Language Model" target="Voice Assistant">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Voice assistants require personalized language models to execute personalized voice tasks like calling contacts or playing music.</data>
      <data key="d6">task dependency, user customization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7994dd4a5f0c1996cedd50a0545ebc9a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Personalized Language Model" target="Calling Domain">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The calling domain utilizes personalized language models to match spoken names against individual contact lists.</data>
      <data key="d6">personalization, domain application</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cdcec1aad44f0e059edddb969db938a4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Personalized Language Model" target="Out-Of-Vocabulary Words">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Personalized language models address out-of-vocabulary contact names by utilizing monophone representations instead of dynamic lexicon updates.</data>
      <data key="d6">vocabulary expansion, pronunciation modeling</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cdcec1aad44f0e059edddb969db938a4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Class Language Model" target="Two-Layer Pre-Initialization Mechanism">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The two-layer pre-initialization mechanism leverages class language models with class tags to isolate dynamic components from public transitions.</data>
      <data key="d6">architecture integration, model optimization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7994dd4a5f0c1996cedd50a0545ebc9a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Class Language Model" target="FST Replacement Algorithm">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The FST replacement algorithm is used to replace class label placeholders in generic class language models with personalized user transducers.</data>
      <data key="d6">transducer manipulation, model replacement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7994dd4a5f0c1996cedd50a0545ebc9a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Class Language Model" target="Finite-State Transducer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Class language models are represented and implemented using finite-state transducer structures such as root FSTs and class FSTs.</data>
      <data key="d6">representation, implementation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dacf09330ac9f65bece416e803b0daa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Class Language Model" target="Pre-Composition Algorithm">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The pre-composition algorithm is adapted for class language models by restricting composition to shared non-personalized states.</data>
      <data key="d6">algorithm adaptation, optimization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dacf09330ac9f65bece416e803b0daa</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Calling Domain" target="Facebook">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Facebook employees provided the evaluation dataset of 7,500 utterances representing the calling domain.</data>
      <data key="d6">data collection, speech corpus</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cdcec1aad44f0e059edddb969db938a4</data>
    </edge>
