    </edge>
    <edge source="Friedman Test" target="Nemenyi Test">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Nemenyi test is used as a post-hoc test to compare all classifiers against one another following the Friedman test.</data>
      <data key="d6">statistical post-hoc testing, classifier comparison</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-2cf0072fd347c405b3a96e6f40b88278</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Friedman Test" target="Bonferroni-Dunn Test">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Bonferroni-Dunn test is a post-hoc procedure applied after the Friedman test when comparing classifiers against a control model.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-hoc testing, hypothesis testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-2cf0072fd347c405b3a96e6f40b88278</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nemenyi Test" target="Bonferroni-Dunn Test">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both Bonferroni-Dunn and Nemenyi tests are post-hoc statistical procedures following the Friedman test, differing in whether they compare against a control classifier or all classifiers to each other.</data>
      <data key="d6">statistical methods, comparison</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-2cf0072fd347c405b3a96e6f40b88278</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nemenyi Test" target="Bonferroni-Dunn Post-Hoc Test">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both are post-hoc statistical tests used after rejecting the null hypothesis, with the Nemenyi test comparing all classifiers against each other rather than against a single control.</data>
      <data key="d6">statistical testing, methodological comparison</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-2e9d6cd8b1a1f2b1ece006c42d145e4d</data>
